5600:
** εἰμί**
(**sum**), _Aeolic dialect_ **ἔμμι** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.15, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.32}}; Cretan dialect **ἠμί** {{Refs|“GDI” 4959a}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.388, +others, _Aeolic dialect_ ἔσσι}}, _Epic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ ἐσσί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.176, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.90, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 134; ἐσί “GDI” 4959a}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐστί, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(1).677 (from Rhodes), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.17, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἐστόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.112}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐσμέν, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰμέν (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.60), ἐμέν 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 294}}, _Doric dialect_ εἰμές {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.73, but ἠμέν “GDI” 5178.34}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἰσί (-ίν), _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἔασι (-ιν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.73, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 8.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 29, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.84}}, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.24, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.45, “IG” 9(1).32.22 (from Phocis), etc.: _imperative_ ἴσθι (ἔσθι 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 361 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus)}}, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also in _middle_ form ἔσσο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.200, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.28, Macedonius Lyricus “Paean” 31, late Prose ἔσο 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.241d, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 3.5, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.12, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 34}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔστω (ἤτω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.103.31, and late Inscrr., “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2664, +others; but in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c to be read ἴτω), _Doric dialect_ εἴτω, ἤτω, 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1411.21, Elean ἤστω “Schwyzer” 424}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔστωσαν, but ἔστων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 502a, ὄντων [prev. author] “Lg.” 879b}}, and early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12.22, etc. (ἔστωσαν first in 2nd c.BC, [prev. work]22.1328), _Doric dialect_ ἐόντων [prev. work]1126:}}_subjunctive_ ὦ, ᾖς, ᾖ, _Epic dialect_ ἔω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.18}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.300, +others (also ἔῃσι 2.366, +others, ᾖσι (ν) 19.202, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 294)}}, also _Boeotian dialect_ ἔνθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3172.165, μετείω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.47 and perhaps εἴῃ 9.245, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὦντι {{Refs|“SIG” 940.3 (from Crete), ἔωντι “GDI” 5040.14 (from Hierapytna)}}, _Boeotian dialect_ ἴωνθι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3171.46 (3rd c.BC): _optative_ εἴην, -ης (εἴησθα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 715)}}, -η, also ἔοις, ἔοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.284, 142, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἴοισαν Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. {{Refs|1911.133 (from Gonni)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ εἴτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 149e, “Sph.” 243e}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ εἶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 921 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ εἶτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.195}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἶεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.372, etc., εἴησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, etc.}}; Elean ἔα, ={εἴη}, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9 (6th c.BC), and σύν-εαν, ={συνεῖεν}, “GDI” 1149 (6th c.BC):}}]_infinitive_ εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἦναι “SIG” 306.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔμμεναι (also _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμμεν᾽ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 34), ἔμμεν (also 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.42, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 623 (Lyric poetry)), ἔμεναι, ἔμεν, also ἔμειν “SIG” 1166 (from Dodona)}}; _Doric dialect_ εἶμεν Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77, 79, “IG” 7.1.7 (from Megara), ἦμεν “Test.Epict.” 5.16, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.75, Cretan dialect ἦμεν or ἤμην “Leg.Gort.” 1.15, +others, “GDI” 4998i 2, +others, Megarian dialect εἴμεναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 775, εἴμειν “IG” 12(1).155.100 (from Rhodes), 14.952 (from Agrigentum); εἶν [prev. work] 12(9).211.10 (from Eretria), “SIG” 135.4 (from Olynthus), etc.:}}_participle_ ὤν, _Epic dialect_ ἐών, ἐοῦσα, ἐόν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria ἰών “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.23 H.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ _feminine_ ἰῶσα {{Refs|“IG” 7.3172.116 (Orchom.)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ _feminine_ ἔσσα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 75.4, “IG” 4.952.2 (Epid.), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.16, ἐοῖσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.265, ἔασσα “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 9, Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62, εὖσα Erinna Lyrica 5.5}} (also _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.16, εὔντων 2.85), ἐᾶσα 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96d, “IG” 5(1).1470.8 (from Messene), ἴαττα “Leg.Gort.” 8.47; _accusative_ _sing._ εὖντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.3}}; _nominative_ _singular_ εἴς in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1756.13, _plural_ ἔντες “Tab.Heracl.” 1.117; _dative_ _plural_ ἔντασσι [prev. work]104; _genitive_ _plural_ παρ-έντων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 64}}: _imperfect_ ἦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.77, etc., _Epic dialect_ ἔον}} (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 127, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787 “fragment” 3 ii 21), in _Attic dialect_ ἦ}} (uncertain in _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc., but usually altered to ἦν in codices (and ἦν}} is required by metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 280), _contraction_ from _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἦα (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.808, +others, “IG” 12(8).449.2 (from Thasos)}}, whence Hom.and later _Ionic dialect_ ἔᾱ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.321, +others, ἔας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, ἔατε [prev. author] 4.119)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦεν, always with ν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; ἔην as _1st pers._ _singular_, only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.762 (assuming variant, +others ἔον), frequently as _3rd.pers._ _sing._ (generally before a consonant, so that ἔεν is possible), sometimes also ἤην}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσθα, later ἦς (which is variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.26), sometimes in LXX (LXX.Judg.11.35, LXX.Ruth.3.2, +others), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e, Erinna Lyrica 4.4, NT.Matt.25.21, +others, ἦσθας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 156, _Epic dialect_ ἔησθα}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦν, _Epic dialect_ ἔην, ἤην, ἦεν (see. above), _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ ἦς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 30.1, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 102, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.90, “SIG” 241.145 (Delph.)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἤστην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 387, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 982, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ἦμες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἦτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 276c, ἦστε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 821, “Ec.” 1086}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἦσαν, _Ionic dialect_ and poetical ἔσαν (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321, 825, ἦν is not _plural_ for ἦσαν}}, but is rather a peculiarity of syntax, see below see, but is _3rd.pers._ _plural_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, +others, “SIG” 560.15 (Epidamnus, 3rd c.BC))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔον {{NT+5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 644.12; later ἤμην “PSI” 4.362.21 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 527.46 (Crete, 3rd c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.1740 (from Cyme), always in LXX as LXX.Bar.1.19, cf. NT.Matt.23.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.174a, etc., and sometimes in codices of earlier writers, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.34, “Tragica Adespota” 124 (cited from 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 931 by 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus and from [prev. author] “Tr.” 474 by Aps.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.9, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 26}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσο {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 379 (from Aezani)}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦστο {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 1.455.7 (from Phrygia)}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἤμεθα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.11 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Bar.1.19, LXX.1Ki.25.16, NT.Eph.2.3}}; _subjunctive_ ὦμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PBaden” 48.12 (2nd c.BC), ἦται “GDI” , ἦνται probably in “IG” 5(1).1390.83 (from Andania)}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ also ἔσκον, used by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 656 (Lyric poetry):}}_future_ ἔσομαι, ἔσται, _Epic dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ also ἔσσομαι, ἔσεται, ἔσσετα; _Aeolic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔσσῃ probably in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 67, 87; _Doric dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐσσῇ, ἐσσεῖται, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.393, 13.317, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.5, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐσσοῦνται Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 codices (but ἔσσονται “Tab.Heracl.” 1.113)}}, _infinitive_ ἐσσεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 57.}} —All forms of the _present_ _indicative_ are enclitic (except _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ and _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔασι); but _3rd.pers._ _singular_ is written ἔστι when it begins a sentence or verse, or when it immediately follows οὐκ, καί, εἰ, ὡς, ἀλλά, or τοῦτ᾽, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.553 (also μή _accusative_ to “Etymologicum Magnum” 301.3)}}; later Grammars wrote ἔστι as _substantive_ Verb, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 880.22.}}
**A** as the _substantive_ Verb,
**A.I** of persons, **exist**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὐδ᾽ ἔσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.437}}; ἔτ᾽ εἰσί they **are** still **in being**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.433, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 445, etc.; τεθνηῶτος.. μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐόντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.289}}; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί he **is** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1162}}; οὐδὲ δὴν ἦν he **was** not long-**lived**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.131}}; ὁ οὐκ ὤν, οἱ οὐκ ὄντες, of **those who are** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, 44}}; οἱ ὄντες the **living**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.29.2}}; ὁ ὤν the {{LXX+8th c.BC+|“Eternal”, LXX.Exo.3.14, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.289; θεοὶ αἰὲν ἐόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290}}; ἐσσόμενοι **posterity**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.119; κἀγὼ γὰρ ἦ ποτ᾽, ἀλλὰ νῦν οὐκ εἴμ᾽ ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 284}}; ὡς ἂν εἶεν ἅνθρωποι **might continue in being**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190c; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 318b25}}; of things, εἰ ἔστι ἀληθέως [ἡ τράπεζα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17, etc.}}; of cities, ὄλωλεν, οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ Τροία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1292, compare “Heracl.” 491}}; δοκεῖ μοι Καρχηδόνα μὴ εἶναι **censeo Carthaginem esse delendam**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 27}}; ἂν ᾖ τὸ στράτευμα **be in existence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.17}}; of money, **to be in hand**, τῶν ὄντων χρημάτων καὶ τῶν προσιόντων {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.25}}; τὰ ὄντα **property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24, etc.}}; τὸ ἐσόμενον ἐκ.. **future revenue** from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 46.420 (Olymos, 1st c.BC)}}; of place, τὴν οὖσαν ἐκκλησίαν the **local** church, {{NT|NT.Act.13.1}}; of time, τοῦ ὄντος μηνός in the **current** month, {{Refs|“BGU” 146.4, etc.}}; **in office**, ἱερέων τῶν ὄντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 5.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; αἱ οὖσαι [ἐξουσίαι] the powers **that be, Ep. Rom.**{{Refs|13.1.}}
**A.II** of the **real** world, **be**, opposed to become, γίγνεται πάντα ἃ δή φαμεν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, etc.}}; τὸ ὄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Being”, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.35, 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27d, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ μὴ ὄν, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.; οὐδὲν γίνεται ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U.; ἐξ οὐκ ὄντων ἐποίησεν αὐτὰ ὁ θεός LXX.2Mac.7.28}}; τὰ ὄντα the **world of things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 7, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.5, etc.}}; ὄν_indeclinable_, τῶν ὂν εἰδῶν species **of Being**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.10.}}
**A.II.2** of circumstances, events, etc., **to happen**, τά τ᾽ ἐόντα, τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα, πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.70; ἡ ἐσβολὴ ἔμελλεν ἔσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, etc.}}; τῆς προδοσίας οὔσης since treachery **was there**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.103}}; ἕως ἂν ὁ πόλεμος ᾖ so long as it **last**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.58; αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐνιαυτὸν ἔσονται [prev. author] 4.118}}; τί ἐστι; what **is** it? what's the matter? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 193}}; τί οὖν ἦν τοῦτ; how **came** it **to pass**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a: repeated with a _relative_ to avoid a positive assertion, ἔστι δ᾽ ὅπῃ νῦν ἔ.}} things **are** as they **are**, i.e. are ill, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 67.}}
**A.III** **be the fact** or **the case**, διπλασίαν ἂν τὴν δύναμιν εἰκάζεσθαι ἤ ἐστιν twice as large as it **really is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστι καλόν beauty **in its essence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211c, compare “Phd.” 74b}}; frequently in _participle_, τὸν ἐόντα λόγον λέγειν or φαίνειν the **true** story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 116}}; τῷ ἐόντι χρήσασθαι tell the **truth**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 30; τὰ ὄντα ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8}}; σκῆψιν οὐκ οὖσαν, λόγον οὐκ ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 584, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1052}}; τῷ ὄντι **in reality, in fact**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d, etc.}}; to apply a quotation to a case in point, τῷ ὄντι κλαυσίγελως **real** 'smiles through tears' (with allusion to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.484), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196d}}; κατὰ τὸ ἐόν according to the **fact, right**ly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97}}; πᾶν τὸ ἐόν the whole **truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.11; τοῦ ἐόντος ἀποτεύξεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2.}}
**A.IV** followed by the _relative_, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅς or ὅστις **no one**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὃς.. ἀπαλάλκοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.348; οὐκ ἔ. οὐδεὶς ὅς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 903}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅτῳ, ={οὐδενί}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989: frequently in _plural_, εἰσὶν οἵ}}, = Latin _sunt qui_, used exactly like{ἔνιοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77d, “Grg.” 503a, etc. (εἰσί τινες οἵ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24)}}; ἐστὶν ἃ χωρία, πολίσματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.12, 65; ἐστὶν ἃ εἰπεῖν [prev. author] 2.67; ἦσαν οἵ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14}}; the _singular_ Verb is used even with _masculine_ and _feminine_ _plural_, ἐστὶν οἵ, αἵ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1, “VC” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.16}}; more frequently in oblique cases, ποταμῶν ἐστὶ ὧν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.187; ἐστὶν ἀφ᾽ ὧν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65}}; ἐστὶ παρ᾽ οἷς, ἐστὶν ἐν οἷς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.23, 5.25: in questions ὅστις is used, ἔστιν ἥντινα δόξαν.. ἀπεκρίνατ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85b: with _relative_ Particles, ἐστὶν ἔνθα}}, = Latin _est ubi_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.15, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅπῃ, ἔσθ᾽ ὅπου, **some**how, **some**where, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 331d, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 517, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448, etc.}}; in questions expecting a _negative_ answer, ἐ. ὁπόθεν, ὅπω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 35a, “R.” 493e, etc.; οὐ γάρ ἐσθ᾽ ὅπως 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅπως οὐ **in any case, necessarily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 188; οὐκ ἔ. ὡς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76e, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅτε, ἔσθ᾽ ὅτε, **some**times, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 180.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, etc.}}
**A.V** ἦν is sometimes used with _plural_ _masculine_ and _feminine_, usually at the beginning of a sentence, **there was**, τῆς δ᾽ ἦν τρεῖς κεφαλαί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321; (but in ἦν δ᾽ ἐρῳδιοί τε πολλοί 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, compare 59, +others, it may be taken as _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_)}}; ἦν δ᾽ ἀμφίπλεκτοι κλίμακες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 520 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἦν ἄρα κἀκεῖνοι ταλακάρδιοι epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.184; less frequently ἔστι, ἔστι δὲ μεταξὺ.. ἑπτὰ στάδιοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, compare 7.34; ἔστι.. ἄρχοντές τε καὶ δῆμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463a}}; before dual Nouns, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500d.}}
**A.VI** ἔστι _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, **it is possible**, ἔστι γὰρ ἀμφοτέροισιν ὀνείδεα μυθήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.246; ἔστι μὲν εὕδειν, ἔστι δὲ τερπομένοισιν ἀκούειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.392}}; εἴ τί πού ἐστι (i.e. πιθέσθαι){{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.193; τοιάδε.. ἐστὶν ἀκοῦσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1055 (_anapaest meter_); ἔστι τεκμήρια ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1163, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.105, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.6, etc.}}; so in _imperative_, _optative_, and _subjunctive_, ἔστω ἀποφέρεσθαι τῷ βουλομένῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.7; μυρία ἂν εἴη λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; ὅπως ἂν ᾖ δρᾶν “IG” 2.1054.91: more frequently in _negative_ clauses, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.267, etc.}}; followed by ὥστε with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 656}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἁδόντα δ᾽ εἴη με τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ὁμιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.96; ἔστιν ἐκπεσεῖν ἀρχῆς Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 757}}: sometimes not _impersonal_ in this sense, θάλασσα δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἦν ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 419.}}
**A.VI.b** ἔστω in argument, **let it be granted**, ἔστω τοῦτο ἀληθὲς εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; ἔστω σοι τοῦθ᾽ οὕτως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.987b; ἔστω εἶναί τινα τοιοῦτον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr.74.24.}}
**B** most frequently, **to be**, the Copula connecting the predicate with the Subject, both being in the same case: hence, **signify, import**, τὸ γὰρ εἴρειν λέγειν ἐστίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398d}}; especially in the phrase τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι, **hoc est**; Σκαιόλαν, ὅπερ ἐστὶ Λαϊόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 17: with numerals, τὰ δὶς πέντε δέκα ἐστίν}} twice five **are** ten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.7}}; εἶναί τις or τι, **to be** somebody, something, **be** of some consequence, see at {τι; οὐδὲν εἶναι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.}}
**B.2** _periphrastic_ with the Participle to represent the finite Verb: with _perfect_ _participle_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τετληότες εἰμέν, for τετλήκαμεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.873}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἦν τεθνηκώς, for ἐτεθνήκει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 869}}; ἔσται δεδορκώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1179; εἰμὶ γεγώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1299; πεφυκός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1473; δεδρακότες εἰσίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68; κατακεκονότες ἔσεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.36}}: with _aorist_ _participle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βλήμενος ἦν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.211}}; so προδείσας εἰμί, οὐ σιωπήσας ἔσε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 90, 1146, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 460}}: with _present_ _participle_, ἦν προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 371; φεύγων Ὀρέστης ἐστίν [prev. author] “Ch.” 136; εἴην οὐκ ἂν εὖ φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1330}}; τί δ᾽ ἐστί.. φέρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 991, compare 274, 708; λέγων ἐστίν τις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1179; ἦν τίς σ᾽ ὑβρίζων [prev. author] “HF” 313; πόρρω ἤδη εἶ πορευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204b; βαδίζων εἰμί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 36; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἦσαν ἱέντες 1.57, +others; even εἰσὶ διάφοροι ἐόντες 3.49 (assuming variant):}} — if the _Article_ is joined with the Part., the noun is made emphatic, Κᾶρές εἰσι οἱ καταδέξαντες the persons who showed her **were** Carians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.171; αὐτὸς ἦν ὁ μαρτυρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798; δόλος ἦν ὁ φράσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 197 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**C** εἶναι is frequently modified in sense by the addition of _adverbs_, or the cases of Nouns without or with Preps.:
**C.I** εἶναι with _adverbs_, where the _adverb_ often merely represents a Noun and stands as the predicate, ἅλις δέ οἱ ἦσαν ἄρουραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.122, etc.}}; ἀκέων, ἀκήν εἶναι, **to be** silent,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.82; σῖγα πᾶς ἔστω λεώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 532; διαγνῶναι χαλεπῶς ἦν ἄνδρα ἕκαστον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.424}}; ἀσφαλέως ἡ κομιδὴ ἔσται **will go on** safely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}; ἐγγύς, πόρρω εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e: frequently _impersonal_ with words implying good or ill fortune, Κουρήτεσσι κακῶς ἦν}} **it fared** ill with them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.551; εὖ γὰρ ἔσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1188, etc.; ἡδέως ἂν αὐτοῖς εἴη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.30.}}
**C.II** with _genitive_, to express descent or extraction, πατρὸς δ᾽ εἴμ᾽ ἀγαθοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.109; αἵματός εἰς ἀγαθοῖο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71, etc.; πόλεως μεγίστης εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.19.}}
**C.II.b** to express the material of which a thing is made, ἡ κρηπίς ἐστι λίθων μεγάλων **consists of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93; τῆς πόλιος ἐούσης δύο φαρσέων [prev. work] 186}}; τοιούτων ἔργων ἐστὶ ἡ τυραννίς **is made up of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.ή, etc.
**C.II.c** to express the class to which a person or thing belongs, εἶ γὰρ τῶν φίλων **you are one of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 345; ἐτύγχανε βουλῆς ὤν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; ὅσοι ἦσαν τῶν προτέρων στρατιωτῶν [prev. author] 7.44; Κριτίας τῶν τριάκοντα ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31}}; ἔστι τῶν αἰσχρῶν **it is in the class of** disgraceful things, i. e. **it is** disgraceful, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2.}}
**C.II.d** to express that a thing belongs to another, Τροίαν Ἀχαιῶν οὖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 269; τὸ πεδίον ἦν μέν κοτε Χορασμίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, etc.}}: hence, **to be of the party of**, ἦσαν.. τινὲς μὲν φιλίππου, τινὲς δὲ τοῦ βελτίστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.56, compare 37.53}}; **to be de pendent upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 737, etc.}}; **to be at the mercy of**, ἔστι τοῦ λέγοντος, ἢν φόβους λέγῃ {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 917.}}
**C.II.e** to express one's duty, business, custom, nature, and the like, οὔτοι γυναικός ἐστι **'tis** not a woman's **part**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 940; τὸ ἐπιτιμᾶν παντὸς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16}}; τὸ δὲ ναυτικὸν τέχνης ἐστίν **is matter of** art, **requires** art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 83.}}
**C.II.f** in LXX, **to be occupied about**, ἦσαν τοῦ θύειν {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.30.17}}; ἔσεσθαι, with _genitive_, **to be about to**, ἐσόμεθα τοῦ σῶσαί σε {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.10.11.}}
**C.III** with the _dative_, ἐστί μοι I **have**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}
**C.III.2** with two datives., σφίσι τε καὶ Ἀθηναίοισι εἶναι οὐδὲν πρῆγμα that they and the Athenians **have** nothing **to do** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84; μηδὲν εἶναι σοὶ καὶ φιλίππῳ πρᾶγμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; more shortly, σοί τε καὶ τούτοισι πρήγμασι τί ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33}}; τί τῷ νόμῳ καὶ τῇ βασάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.36}}; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σο; Latin _quid tecum est mihi?_ {{NT|NT.Mark.5.7, etc.}}; also ἐμοὶ οὐδὲν πρὸς τοὺς τοιούτους (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.12; ἐν οἷς πρὸς τοὺς ἐναντίους ἐστὶ τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.278}}; ἔσται αὐτῳ πρὸς τὸν θεόν, in tomb inscriptions, {{Refs|“JHS” 18.113, etc.}}
**C.III.3** with ἄσμενος, βουλόμενος, etc., added, ἐμοὶ δέ κεν ἀσμένῳ εἴη **'twould be** to my delight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.108; οὐκ ἂν σφίσι βουλομένοις εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35; προσδεχομένῳ [prev. author] 6.46; θέλοντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1356 (Lyric poetry); ἡδομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187c.}}
**C.IV** with Preps., εἶναι ἀπό τινος, ={εἶναί τινος}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(above 11.a), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.9; εἰσὶν ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίων αὗται πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12d}}; but εἶναι ἀπ᾽ οἴκου **to be** away from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99.}}
**C.IV.2** εἶναι ἔκ τινος **to be sprung** from, εἴμ᾽ ἐκ Παιονίης, Μυρμιδόνων ἔξ εἰμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.154, 24.397, etc.}}; ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης **it is** of necessity, i. e. necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 256d.}}
**C.IV.3** εἶναι ἐν.. **to be** in a certain state, ἐν εὐπαθείῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; ἐν ἀθυμία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, etc.; ἐν ταραχαῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218}}; εἶναι ἐν ἀξιώματι **to be** in esteem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130}}; οἱ ἐν τέλεϊ ἐόντες those in office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.18, etc.}}; but εἶναι ἐν τέχνῃ, ἐν φιλοσοφία **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a.}}
**C.IV.3.b** ἐν σοί ἐστι **it depends** on thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 963; ἐν σοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν [prev. author] “OT” 314; so also ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Ph.” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2, etc.}}
**C.IV.4** εἶναι διά.., much like{εἶναι ἐν}.., εἶναι διὰ φόβου, ={φοβεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}; εἶναι δι᾽ ὄχλου, ={ὀχληρὸν εἶναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.73; εἶναι διὰ μόχθων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.25}}; εἶναι δι᾽ αἰτίας, ={αἰτιᾶσθαι}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.70}}; Geometry texts, **pass** through, διὰ τᾶς ἑτέρας διαμέτρου ἐόντος τοῦ ἐπιπέδου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 20.}}
**C.IV.5** εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῆς **to be** by oneself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.23}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ ὀνόματος **to bear** a name, {{Refs|[prev. author] 39.21}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τοῖς πράγμασιν **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.12}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινα **to be** against him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.33}}; εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑξήκοντα στάδια **to reach** sixty stadia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τὰς ἁφάς **pass** through the points of contact, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 4.1}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινι, see above 3 b.
**C.IV.6** εἶναι πρός τινος **to be** in one's favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10, 29, etc.}}; **to suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; εἶναι πρός τινι **engaged** in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.31}}; πρὸς τοῖς ἰδίοις **mind** one's own affairs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a6, “Ath.” 16.3; εἶναι πρὸς τὸ κωλύειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.3}}; πρὸς τὸ πονεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Teles p.46 H.; εἶναι περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7, etc.}}
**C.IV.7** εἶναι παρά τινι or τινα, = παρειναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.2.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά}} (assuming variant).
**C.IV.8** εἶναι ὑπό τινα or τινι **to be** subject to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.17 (assuming variant), 6.2.4.}}
**C.IV.9** περὶ τούτων ἐστίν that **is** the question, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 30.}}
**C.IV.10** εἶναι ἀπό.., in Geometry texts, **to be constructed** upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.9, “Con.Sph.” 7.}}
**D** ἐστί is very frequently omitted, mostly in the _present_ _indicative_ before certain predicates, as ἀνάγκη, ἄξιον, δυνατόν, εἰκός, ἕτοιμον, οἷόν τε, ῥᾴδιον, χρεών, etc., and after the _neuter_ of Verbals in -τέος, and such forms as θαυμαστὸν ὅσον: less frequently with other persons and moods, εἰμί omitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 92, “Aj.” 813}}; εἶ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.206}}; ἐσμέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634}}; ἐστέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.463}}; εἰσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 499 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 2.778 B}}; _subjunctive_ ᾖ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.376, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 659, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32}}; _optative_ εἴη, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1183.12}}; _imperfect_ ἦν, {{Refs|[prev. work]2.778 B}}; _future_ ἔσονται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.394.}}
**E** the Inf. frequently seems redundant,
**E.1** in phrases implying power or will to do a thing, ἑκὼν εἶναι (see. ἑκών) κατὰ δύναμιν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.32; εἰς δύναμιν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300c}}; τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐκείνοις εἶναι, **quantum in illis esset**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.58; τὸ ἐπὶ σφᾶς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; τὸ κατὰ τοῦτον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9; κατὰ τοῦτο εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a}}; τὸ τήμερον, τὸ νῦν εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 396e, “La.” 201c, Theopomp. Comedy texts 98, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2, etc.}}
**E.2** after Verbs of naming or choosing, σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσι τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e; σύμμαχόν μιν εἵλοντο εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.134}}; of giving, δῶκε ξεινήϊον εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.20.}}
**F** _imperfect_ ἦνissts. used where other languages take the _present_,
**F.1** after ἄρα, to express a fact which **is and has always been** the same, δέρμα δὲ ἀνθρώπου.. ἦν ἄρα σχεδὸν δερμάτων πάντων λαμπρότατον human skin then **it appears is**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; Κύπρις οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἦν θεός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 359}}; ὡς ἄρ᾽ ἦσθ᾽ ἐμὸς πατὴρ ὀρθῶς{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1169; ἦ πολύμοχθον ἄρ᾽ ἦν γένος.. ἁμερίων [prev. author] “IA” 1330; ἦ στωμύλος ἦσθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.79}}; so also when there is reference to a past thought, τουτὶ τί ἦ; what **is** this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 157, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 387c: so in the Aristotelian formula τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (“APo.” 82b38, +others)}}, used to express **the essential nature** of a thing, where τί ἦν (for ἐστί) takes the place of the _dative_ in such phrases as τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, τὸ μεγέθει εἶναι, {{Refs|“APr.” 67b12, “de An.” 429b10.}}
**G** ἐγώ εἰμι, in {{LXX|LXX, pleonastic for ἐγώ, ἐγώ εἰμι οὐχ ἥμαρτον LXX.Judg.11.27, compare 6.18; also ἔσται πᾶς ἀποκτενεῖ με LXX.Gen.4.14.}}

5602:
** ὧδε**,
_Attic dialect_ also **ὡδί** (which see), _demonstrative_ _adverb_ of ὅδε:
**I** of Manner, **in this wise, thus,** sometimes **so very, so exceedingly,** frequently from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} usually before the Verb, but after it in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.181, 574, 10.91, 18.392; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus and 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus mostly place it after the Verb}}:—in construction ὧδε is answered by ὡς, **so**.., as.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.300, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.312: answering ὥς περ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.478, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 276, etc.: followed by a _relative_, τίς ὧδε τλησικάρδιος, ὅτῳ..; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 160 (Lyric poetry)}}; by εἰ, δοκῶ μοι ὧδε ἂν μᾶλλον πιθέσθαι σοι, εἴ μοι δείξειας.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 391a; εἰ φρονῶν ἔπρασσον, οὐδ᾽ ἂν ὧδ᾽ ἐγιγνόμην κακός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 272}}: with _participle_, δύαις τε καμφθεὶς ὧδε δεσμὰ φυγγάνω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 513: ὧδέπως}} is frequently in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 393d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.21, etc.}}; also in later Prose, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 32, etc.}}
**I.2** of a State or Condition, **as it is,** πρόμολ᾽ ὧ. come forth **just as thou art,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.392, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.182, 2.28}} (rightly so taken by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus cited in Scholia A 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.271, cf. Scholia A 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.392}}: see below {{Refs 8th c.BC+|11); στρεύγεσθαι ὧδ᾽ ἄτως 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.513, compare 10.91.}}
**I.3** of something following, **thus, as follows,** especially to introduce quoted words, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.181 (where it follows the verb), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.111, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 203, etc.; ὧδ᾽ ἠμείψατο 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 378}}: sometimes referring to what goes before, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.2.}}
**I.4** _pleonastic_, τόσον ὧδ᾽ ἐβόησας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.403; ὧδε.. τῇδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1301.}}
**I.5** with _genitive_, γένους μὲν ἥκεις ὧ. τοῖσδε (where ἥκεις ={προσήκεις}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 213.}}
**II** of Place, **hither,**compare ὅδε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1: 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus}} denied this usage in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus altogether (cf. Scholia A 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.271, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus “Lex.”}} see at {ὧδε}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 178.25, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 792.2, +others)}}; and most of the passages taken in this sense may be referred to _signification_{{Refs|1.1 or 2}}, **just as you see,** see above{{Refs|1.2: but}}**hither** seems probably in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.544, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.346, 10.537: examples of ὧδε}} **hither,** are found in Trag. (especially in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, as “OT” 7, 144, 298, “OC” 182 (Lyric poetry), 841 (Lyric poetry), 1206, 1547, “Tr.” 402)}}; also in Comedy texts, as ἴτω τις ὧ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 229 (Lyric poetry); and later, as 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.49; χιλίας ὧ. καὶ χιλίας ὧδ᾽ ἐμβαλεῖν [prev. author] 5.48; ὧ. χὦδε χασκεύσῃ [prev. author] 4.42}}; ὧδε καὶ ὧδε **this way** and **that,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 5.128 (1st c.AD: Automedon Epigrammaticus): προσάγαγε ὧ. τὸν υἱόν σου NT.Luke.9.41.}}
**II.2** **here,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.98}}; εἰσὶν ὧ. πρὸς ἡμᾶς they are **here** with us, [{{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Mark.6.3; ὧ. κἀκεῖ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.34a, cf. NT.Mark.13.21: τηνεῖ δρύες, ὧδε (variant{ἠδὲ})}}] κύπειρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.106, compare 120, 121: so 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 54 (_anapaest meter_) _accusative_ to 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. (but see Kock).}}

5603:
** ᾠδή**, ἡ,
_contraction_ for ἀοιδή, **song, lay, ode,** {{Refs|Hymnus ad Apollinem 20, “hymnus ad Cererem” 494; in Trag. (except that 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus uses only ἀοιδή (which see))}}, of dirges, πολλὰς θρήνων ᾠδάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 88 (_anapaest meter_); ὀξυτόνους ᾠ. θρηνήσει [prev. author] “Aj.” 631 (Lyric poetry); ᾠδὰ ἐπικήδειος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 514 (Lyric poetry)}}; but also of **joyful songs, songs of praise,** καλλίνικος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 865 (Lyric poetry); ἴακχος [prev. author] “Cyc.” 69 (Lyric poetry); λύπας πολυχόρδοις ᾠ. παύειν [prev. author] “Med.” 197 (_anapaest meter_); ᾠδὰς ὑστέροισι θήσετε [prev. author] “Supp.” 1225; χαίροντες ᾠδῆς.. μέλεσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 244 (Lyric poetry); ὑμεναίοις καὶ νυμφιδίοισι δέχεσθ᾽ ᾠ. [prev. author] “Av.” 1729 (Lyric poetry): frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, ᾠ. κιθαρῳδική “Lg.” 722d}}; κιθαρίζειν πρὸς τὴν ᾠ. {{Refs|Alcaeus1.108a}}; ᾠδαὶ καὶ ἡ ἄλλη ποίησις **lyric poetry** and.., {{Refs|“Phdr.” 245a; ἐν ταῖς ᾠδαῖς καὶ μέλεσιν “R.” 399c}}, compare 398c; opposed to λέξις, {{Refs|“Lg.” 816d}}; ἐν ᾠδαῖς καὶ μύθοις καὶ λόγοις {{Refs|[prev. work]664a}}; of poems such as those of Stesichorus on Helen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.64}}; of the various songs associated with particular employments or conditions, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 37, compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1164.10, 1236.60.}}
**2** ={ἐπῳδός}, **magic song, spell,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 2.7.}}
**3** _metonymy_ for χορδή, Τέρπανδρος.. δέκα ζεῦξε Μοῦσαν ἐν ᾠδαῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 238.}}
**II** **singing,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 33, etc.}}; of birds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 613b24.}}

5604:
Included with: ** ὠδίς**
({{Refs|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 211, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 313a)}}, ῖνος, ἡ: _Epic dialect_ _dative_ _plural_ ὠδίνεσσι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 92, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.61, etc.}}: later _nominative_ **ὠδίν** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.37.3, NT.1Thes.5.3}}:— mostly in _plural_, **pangs** or **throes of childbirth,** πικρὰς ὠδῖνας ἔχουσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.271}}; τέκε.. ἐν μόναις ὠδῖσιν.. διδύμων σθένος υἱῶν at a single **birth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.85; πόνους ἐνεγκοῦσ᾽ ἐν ὠδῖσι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 920 (Lyric poetry); ἐν ὠδίνων λοχίαις ἀνάγκαισι [prev. author] “Ba.” 89 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ion” 452 (Lyric poetry); αἱ δἰ ὠδίνων γοναί [prev. author] “Ph.” 355}}: also in _singular_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.43, “N.” 1.36, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 533 (Lyric poetry); γυνὴ φεύγει πικρὰν ὠδῖνα παίδων [prev. author] “fragment” 932.}}
**2** in _singular_ also, **that which is born amid throes, child,** παῖδα, φιλτάτην ἐμοὶ ὠδῖνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” 1418, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.31, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 45}}; θαλλὸν ἱερὸν ἐλαίας, Λατοῦς ὠδῖνα (perhaps ὠδῖνι) φίλαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 1102 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἄπτερον ὠδῖνα τέκνων, of young birds, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 1040}}: _plural_, **children,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 7.549 (Leon. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor)}}; ὀρταλίχων ἁπαλὴ ὠδίς, of eggs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 165; τοῦ ᾠοῦ ἐν ὠδῖνι ὄντος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 560b22}}; ὠ. Θαλάσσας, of Aphrodite, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.386}}; ὠδὶς μελίσσης, of honey, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 5.228, +others}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **travail, anguish,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 211, “Supp.” 770 (both _sing._)}}: also in _plural_, of love, ἐμοὶ πικρὰς ὠδῖνας αὑτοῦ προσβαλὼν ἀποίχεται {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 42, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574a, “Phdr.” 251e: frequently in LXX, LXX.Exo.15.14, +others, NT.Matt.24.8.}}
**II.2** **fruit of** the mind's **travail,** τῆς ἐμῆς ὠ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 25; λόγων ὠδῖνες 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 18.3; ἐπέων 3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 117.}}
**II.3** ὠδῖνες θανάτου, ᾅδου, the **bonds** of death, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.22.6, LXX.Psa.17(18).5, 6}} (due to confusion of Heb. **[hudot]ēbel** 'pang' with **[hudot]èbel** 'cord'),compare {{NT|NT.Act.2.24.}}

5605:
** ὠδίνω** [ῑ],
used by early writers only in _present_: _future_ ὠδῑνήσω {{LXX|LXX.Hab.3.10}}: _aorist_ ὤδῑνα {{LXX+3rd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.561 (Jul.Aeg.), 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.5, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.56d; ὠδίνησα LXX.Psa.7.15:—so}}_aorist_ _middle_ and _passive_, ὠδινησάμην, -ήθην, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Psa.113(114).7, LXX.Pro.8.25}}:—**to have the pains of childbirth, be in travail,** ὡς δ᾽ ὅταν ὠδίνουσαν ἔχῃ βέλος ὀξὺ γυναῖκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.269; ὠδίνειν τρομέω· χαλεπὸν βέλος Εἰλειθυίας 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.29, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 502, “Ec.” 529, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 395e, etc.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **to be in travail of** a child, **bring forth,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1234, LXX.Cant.8.5: of animals, ὠ. νεοττούς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.46: proverbial, ὤδινεν ὄρος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 23.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of any great pain, **to be in travail** or **anguish,** of the Cyclops, στενάχων τε καὶ ὠδίνων ὀδύνῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.415; Κύπριδι “Anthologia Graeca” 7.30 (2nd c.BC: Antipater Sidonius Epigrammaticus)}}; **labour painfully,** ὠδίνουσι μέλισσαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 9.363.22 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus (?))}}; of the mind, **to be in the throes** or **agonies of thought,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 148e, +others}}; κυοῦμέν τε καὶ ὠ. περὶ ἐπιστήμης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 210b; ὑπὲρ δισσῶν μίαν ὠδίνειν ψυχήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 258 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὥστε μ᾽ ὠδίνειν τί φῄς what you mean, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 794, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 644}}; ὠδίνειν εἴς τι **to long painfully** for a thing, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.32}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.21, 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Eclogae” 13.38, “Or.” 4.1: with _accusative_, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.31; ἔξοδον 6th c.AD: Choricius Rhetor 42.20 p.517 F.-R.}}
**II.b** **worry, fuss,** ὠδίνοντα μήποτε λήγειν περὶ τοῦ σώματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407c.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_, **to be in travail with,** συμφορᾶς βάρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 325; τὴν καύχησιν τὴν σοφιστικήν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 93, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.578 (5th c.AD: Leo Philosophus Epigrammaticus) (where ὧν is in the case of the antecedent by attraction).}}
**II.3** Causal, **cause to quiver,** as in travail, φωνὴ βροντῆς ὠδίνησε γῆν {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Sir.43.17(18) codex 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor}}

5606:
** ὦμος**, ὁ:
(see. entry near the end):— **the shoulder with the upper arm** (ὠλένη being **the lower**), ξίφεϊ κληῗδα παρ᾽ ὦμον πλῆξ᾽, ἀπὸ δ᾽ αὐχένος ὦμον ἐέργαθεν ἠδ᾽ ἀπὸ νώτου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.146, compare 8.325, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.62; μεταφρένῳ ἐν δόρυ πῆξεν ὤμων μεσσηγύς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.41; τεύχε᾽ ἀπ᾽ ὤμων συλήσειν 15.544; ὦμος στιβαρός, ὦμοι ἴφθιμοι, 5.400, 18.204; εὐρέες 3.210; κυρτώ 2.217; ἀμφ᾽ ὤμοισιν ἔχει σάκος 11.527, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 453; ἐπ᾽ ὤμου.. φέρειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.170, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.39; ἀρεῖτ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ὤμου 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.61}}; κατ᾽ ὤμου δεῖρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3; ἐπ᾽ ὤμων πατέρ᾽ ἔχων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 373; τὰ ὦτα ἐπὶ τῶν ὤμων ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 613c; ὤμοισι φόρησεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.11; ἑλὼν.. σάκος ὤμῳ 15.474; ὤμῳ or ὤμοισιν ἔχειν, 14.376, 1.45, +others}}; ὤμοις or ἐπ᾽ ὤμοις φέρειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 454, “Tr” 564; ἔχειν ἀνὰ φαιδίμῳ ὤμῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.128, 23.275; λαβὼν.. ὦμον εἰς ἀριστερόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1381; ἐπ᾽ ὤμοις θεῖναι [prev. author] “Ba.” 755; κίον᾽ οὐρανοῦ.. ὤμοις ἐρείδων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 350}}; ὤμοισι τοῖσι ἐμοῖσι 'by the strength of mine **arms**', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.106}}; ἀποστρέψαι τὸν ὦ. to dislocate **it,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 263 (troch.); ὁ δ᾽ ὦμος.. πιέζεται [prev. author] “Ra.” 30; τὸν ὦμον θλίβομαι [prev. author] “fragment” 323: _plural_ for _sing._, ὤμοις ἀριστεροῖσιν ἀνακλάσας δέρην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1471.}}
**b** **the shoulder** is sometimes more exactly specified as πρυμνότατος or πρυμνὸς ὦμος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.462, 504; νείατος ὦ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.341, 17.310}}; sometimes opposed to χείρ (the arm), χεῖρες ὤμων.. ἐπαΐσσονται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|23.628; τῶν ἑκατὸν μὲν χεῖρες ἀπ᾽ μων ἀΐσσοντο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 150}}; τοὺς ὤμους ἀποταμόντες σὺν τῇσι χερσί (arms) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.62; ἀποταμόντα ἐν τῷ ὤμῳ τὴν χεῖρα [prev. author] 2.121.έ,compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1127, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 493b26.}}
**2** also of animals. as of a horse, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.510, 15.267, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.6}}; of a lion, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 430}}; of a dog, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.1}}; of crabs, {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 296}}; of birds, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.983b}}; of ants, {{Refs|“Geoponica” 13.10.14.}}
**3** **the shoulder,** in a dress, ἐπὶ τῶν ὤμων τῆς ἐπωμίδος {{LXX+4th c.BC+|LXX.Exo.28.12, compare 25(29); ἐπὶ τῷ ὤμῳ τοῦ χιτῶνος ὑποθέντες 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 31.23 codices (ἐπὶ τῇ ᾤᾳ conjecture Haupt).}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of **the parts below the top** or **head** of any thing, especially of **the fork of a vine** (compare ὠμοχάραξ), {{Refs|“Geoponica” 4.12.4}}; of the womb, Heroph. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.596, compare 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 195}}. (Cf. Latin _umerus_ (from ***omesos**), Gothic _ams_ (stem **amsa-**), Sanskrit _áṃsas,_ also _Aeolic dialect_ ἐπ-ομμάδιος, and (non-Greek) ἀμέσω {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}

5607:
** εἰμί**
(**sum**), _Aeolic dialect_ **ἔμμι** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.15, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.32}}; Cretan dialect **ἠμί** {{Refs|“GDI” 4959a}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.388, +others, _Aeolic dialect_ ἔσσι}}, _Epic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ ἐσσί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.176, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.90, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 134; ἐσί “GDI” 4959a}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐστί, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(1).677 (from Rhodes), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.17, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἐστόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.112}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐσμέν, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰμέν (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.60), ἐμέν 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 294}}, _Doric dialect_ εἰμές {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.73, but ἠμέν “GDI” 5178.34}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἰσί (-ίν), _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἔασι (-ιν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.73, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 8.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 29, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.84}}, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.24, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.45, “IG” 9(1).32.22 (from Phocis), etc.: _imperative_ ἴσθι (ἔσθι 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 361 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus)}}, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also in _middle_ form ἔσσο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.200, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.28, Macedonius Lyricus “Paean” 31, late Prose ἔσο 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.241d, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 3.5, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.12, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 34}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔστω (ἤτω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.103.31, and late Inscrr., “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2664, +others; but in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c to be read ἴτω), _Doric dialect_ εἴτω, ἤτω, 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1411.21, Elean ἤστω “Schwyzer” 424}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔστωσαν, but ἔστων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 502a, ὄντων [prev. author] “Lg.” 879b}}, and early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12.22, etc. (ἔστωσαν first in 2nd c.BC, [prev. work]22.1328), _Doric dialect_ ἐόντων [prev. work]1126:}}_subjunctive_ ὦ, ᾖς, ᾖ, _Epic dialect_ ἔω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.18}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.300, +others (also ἔῃσι 2.366, +others, ᾖσι (ν) 19.202, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 294)}}, also _Boeotian dialect_ ἔνθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3172.165, μετείω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.47 and perhaps εἴῃ 9.245, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὦντι {{Refs|“SIG” 940.3 (from Crete), ἔωντι “GDI” 5040.14 (from Hierapytna)}}, _Boeotian dialect_ ἴωνθι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3171.46 (3rd c.BC): _optative_ εἴην, -ης (εἴησθα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 715)}}, -η, also ἔοις, ἔοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.284, 142, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἴοισαν Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. {{Refs|1911.133 (from Gonni)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ εἴτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 149e, “Sph.” 243e}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ εἶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 921 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ εἶτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.195}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἶεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.372, etc., εἴησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, etc.}}; Elean ἔα, ={εἴη}, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9 (6th c.BC), and σύν-εαν, ={συνεῖεν}, “GDI” 1149 (6th c.BC):}}]_infinitive_ εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἦναι “SIG” 306.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔμμεναι (also _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμμεν᾽ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 34), ἔμμεν (also 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.42, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 623 (Lyric poetry)), ἔμεναι, ἔμεν, also ἔμειν “SIG” 1166 (from Dodona)}}; _Doric dialect_ εἶμεν Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77, 79, “IG” 7.1.7 (from Megara), ἦμεν “Test.Epict.” 5.16, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.75, Cretan dialect ἦμεν or ἤμην “Leg.Gort.” 1.15, +others, “GDI” 4998i 2, +others, Megarian dialect εἴμεναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 775, εἴμειν “IG” 12(1).155.100 (from Rhodes), 14.952 (from Agrigentum); εἶν [prev. work] 12(9).211.10 (from Eretria), “SIG” 135.4 (from Olynthus), etc.:}}_participle_ ὤν, _Epic dialect_ ἐών, ἐοῦσα, ἐόν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria ἰών “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.23 H.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ _feminine_ ἰῶσα {{Refs|“IG” 7.3172.116 (Orchom.)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ _feminine_ ἔσσα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 75.4, “IG” 4.952.2 (Epid.), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.16, ἐοῖσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.265, ἔασσα “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 9, Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62, εὖσα Erinna Lyrica 5.5}} (also _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.16, εὔντων 2.85), ἐᾶσα 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96d, “IG” 5(1).1470.8 (from Messene), ἴαττα “Leg.Gort.” 8.47; _accusative_ _sing._ εὖντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.3}}; _nominative_ _singular_ εἴς in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1756.13, _plural_ ἔντες “Tab.Heracl.” 1.117; _dative_ _plural_ ἔντασσι [prev. work]104; _genitive_ _plural_ παρ-έντων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 64}}: _imperfect_ ἦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.77, etc., _Epic dialect_ ἔον}} (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 127, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787 “fragment” 3 ii 21), in _Attic dialect_ ἦ}} (uncertain in _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc., but usually altered to ἦν in codices (and ἦν}} is required by metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 280), _contraction_ from _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἦα (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.808, +others, “IG” 12(8).449.2 (from Thasos)}}, whence Hom.and later _Ionic dialect_ ἔᾱ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.321, +others, ἔας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, ἔατε [prev. author] 4.119)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦεν, always with ν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; ἔην as _1st pers._ _singular_, only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.762 (assuming variant, +others ἔον), frequently as _3rd.pers._ _sing._ (generally before a consonant, so that ἔεν is possible), sometimes also ἤην}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσθα, later ἦς (which is variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.26), sometimes in LXX (LXX.Judg.11.35, LXX.Ruth.3.2, +others), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e, Erinna Lyrica 4.4, NT.Matt.25.21, +others, ἦσθας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 156, _Epic dialect_ ἔησθα}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦν, _Epic dialect_ ἔην, ἤην, ἦεν (see. above), _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ ἦς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 30.1, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 102, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.90, “SIG” 241.145 (Delph.)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἤστην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 387, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 982, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ἦμες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἦτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 276c, ἦστε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 821, “Ec.” 1086}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἦσαν, _Ionic dialect_ and poetical ἔσαν (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321, 825, ἦν is not _plural_ for ἦσαν}}, but is rather a peculiarity of syntax, see below see, but is _3rd.pers._ _plural_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, +others, “SIG” 560.15 (Epidamnus, 3rd c.BC))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔον {{NT+5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 644.12; later ἤμην “PSI” 4.362.21 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 527.46 (Crete, 3rd c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.1740 (from Cyme), always in LXX as LXX.Bar.1.19, cf. NT.Matt.23.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.174a, etc., and sometimes in codices of earlier writers, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.34, “Tragica Adespota” 124 (cited from 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 931 by 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus and from [prev. author] “Tr.” 474 by Aps.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.9, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 26}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσο {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 379 (from Aezani)}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦστο {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 1.455.7 (from Phrygia)}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἤμεθα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.11 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Bar.1.19, LXX.1Ki.25.16, NT.Eph.2.3}}; _subjunctive_ ὦμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PBaden” 48.12 (2nd c.BC), ἦται “GDI” , ἦνται probably in “IG” 5(1).1390.83 (from Andania)}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ also ἔσκον, used by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 656 (Lyric poetry):}}_future_ ἔσομαι, ἔσται, _Epic dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ also ἔσσομαι, ἔσεται, ἔσσετα; _Aeolic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔσσῃ probably in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 67, 87; _Doric dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐσσῇ, ἐσσεῖται, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.393, 13.317, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.5, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐσσοῦνται Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 codices (but ἔσσονται “Tab.Heracl.” 1.113)}}, _infinitive_ ἐσσεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 57.}} —All forms of the _present_ _indicative_ are enclitic (except _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ and _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔασι); but _3rd.pers._ _singular_ is written ἔστι when it begins a sentence or verse, or when it immediately follows οὐκ, καί, εἰ, ὡς, ἀλλά, or τοῦτ᾽, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.553 (also μή _accusative_ to “Etymologicum Magnum” 301.3)}}; later Grammars wrote ἔστι as _substantive_ Verb, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 880.22.}}
**A** as the _substantive_ Verb,
**A.I** of persons, **exist**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὐδ᾽ ἔσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.437}}; ἔτ᾽ εἰσί they **are** still **in being**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.433, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 445, etc.; τεθνηῶτος.. μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐόντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.289}}; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί he **is** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1162}}; οὐδὲ δὴν ἦν he **was** not long-**lived**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.131}}; ὁ οὐκ ὤν, οἱ οὐκ ὄντες, of **those who are** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, 44}}; οἱ ὄντες the **living**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.29.2}}; ὁ ὤν the {{LXX+8th c.BC+|“Eternal”, LXX.Exo.3.14, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.289; θεοὶ αἰὲν ἐόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290}}; ἐσσόμενοι **posterity**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.119; κἀγὼ γὰρ ἦ ποτ᾽, ἀλλὰ νῦν οὐκ εἴμ᾽ ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 284}}; ὡς ἂν εἶεν ἅνθρωποι **might continue in being**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190c; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 318b25}}; of things, εἰ ἔστι ἀληθέως [ἡ τράπεζα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17, etc.}}; of cities, ὄλωλεν, οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ Τροία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1292, compare “Heracl.” 491}}; δοκεῖ μοι Καρχηδόνα μὴ εἶναι **censeo Carthaginem esse delendam**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 27}}; ἂν ᾖ τὸ στράτευμα **be in existence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.17}}; of money, **to be in hand**, τῶν ὄντων χρημάτων καὶ τῶν προσιόντων {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.25}}; τὰ ὄντα **property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24, etc.}}; τὸ ἐσόμενον ἐκ.. **future revenue** from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 46.420 (Olymos, 1st c.BC)}}; of place, τὴν οὖσαν ἐκκλησίαν the **local** church, {{NT|NT.Act.13.1}}; of time, τοῦ ὄντος μηνός in the **current** month, {{Refs|“BGU” 146.4, etc.}}; **in office**, ἱερέων τῶν ὄντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 5.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; αἱ οὖσαι [ἐξουσίαι] the powers **that be, Ep. Rom.**{{Refs|13.1.}}
**A.II** of the **real** world, **be**, opposed to become, γίγνεται πάντα ἃ δή φαμεν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, etc.}}; τὸ ὄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Being”, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.35, 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27d, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ μὴ ὄν, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.; οὐδὲν γίνεται ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U.; ἐξ οὐκ ὄντων ἐποίησεν αὐτὰ ὁ θεός LXX.2Mac.7.28}}; τὰ ὄντα the **world of things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 7, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.5, etc.}}; ὄν_indeclinable_, τῶν ὂν εἰδῶν species **of Being**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.10.}}
**A.II.2** of circumstances, events, etc., **to happen**, τά τ᾽ ἐόντα, τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα, πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.70; ἡ ἐσβολὴ ἔμελλεν ἔσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, etc.}}; τῆς προδοσίας οὔσης since treachery **was there**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.103}}; ἕως ἂν ὁ πόλεμος ᾖ so long as it **last**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.58; αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐνιαυτὸν ἔσονται [prev. author] 4.118}}; τί ἐστι; what **is** it? what's the matter? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 193}}; τί οὖν ἦν τοῦτ; how **came** it **to pass**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a: repeated with a _relative_ to avoid a positive assertion, ἔστι δ᾽ ὅπῃ νῦν ἔ.}} things **are** as they **are**, i.e. are ill, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 67.}}
**A.III** **be the fact** or **the case**, διπλασίαν ἂν τὴν δύναμιν εἰκάζεσθαι ἤ ἐστιν twice as large as it **really is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστι καλόν beauty **in its essence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211c, compare “Phd.” 74b}}; frequently in _participle_, τὸν ἐόντα λόγον λέγειν or φαίνειν the **true** story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 116}}; τῷ ἐόντι χρήσασθαι tell the **truth**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 30; τὰ ὄντα ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8}}; σκῆψιν οὐκ οὖσαν, λόγον οὐκ ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 584, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1052}}; τῷ ὄντι **in reality, in fact**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d, etc.}}; to apply a quotation to a case in point, τῷ ὄντι κλαυσίγελως **real** 'smiles through tears' (with allusion to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.484), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196d}}; κατὰ τὸ ἐόν according to the **fact, right**ly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97}}; πᾶν τὸ ἐόν the whole **truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.11; τοῦ ἐόντος ἀποτεύξεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2.}}
**A.IV** followed by the _relative_, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅς or ὅστις **no one**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὃς.. ἀπαλάλκοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.348; οὐκ ἔ. οὐδεὶς ὅς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 903}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅτῳ, ={οὐδενί}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989: frequently in _plural_, εἰσὶν οἵ}}, = Latin _sunt qui_, used exactly like{ἔνιοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77d, “Grg.” 503a, etc. (εἰσί τινες οἵ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24)}}; ἐστὶν ἃ χωρία, πολίσματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.12, 65; ἐστὶν ἃ εἰπεῖν [prev. author] 2.67; ἦσαν οἵ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14}}; the _singular_ Verb is used even with _masculine_ and _feminine_ _plural_, ἐστὶν οἵ, αἵ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1, “VC” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.16}}; more frequently in oblique cases, ποταμῶν ἐστὶ ὧν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.187; ἐστὶν ἀφ᾽ ὧν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65}}; ἐστὶ παρ᾽ οἷς, ἐστὶν ἐν οἷς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.23, 5.25: in questions ὅστις is used, ἔστιν ἥντινα δόξαν.. ἀπεκρίνατ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85b: with _relative_ Particles, ἐστὶν ἔνθα}}, = Latin _est ubi_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.15, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅπῃ, ἔσθ᾽ ὅπου, **some**how, **some**where, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 331d, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 517, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448, etc.}}; in questions expecting a _negative_ answer, ἐ. ὁπόθεν, ὅπω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 35a, “R.” 493e, etc.; οὐ γάρ ἐσθ᾽ ὅπως 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅπως οὐ **in any case, necessarily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 188; οὐκ ἔ. ὡς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76e, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅτε, ἔσθ᾽ ὅτε, **some**times, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 180.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, etc.}}
**A.V** ἦν is sometimes used with _plural_ _masculine_ and _feminine_, usually at the beginning of a sentence, **there was**, τῆς δ᾽ ἦν τρεῖς κεφαλαί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321; (but in ἦν δ᾽ ἐρῳδιοί τε πολλοί 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, compare 59, +others, it may be taken as _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_)}}; ἦν δ᾽ ἀμφίπλεκτοι κλίμακες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 520 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἦν ἄρα κἀκεῖνοι ταλακάρδιοι epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.184; less frequently ἔστι, ἔστι δὲ μεταξὺ.. ἑπτὰ στάδιοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, compare 7.34; ἔστι.. ἄρχοντές τε καὶ δῆμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463a}}; before dual Nouns, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500d.}}
**A.VI** ἔστι _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, **it is possible**, ἔστι γὰρ ἀμφοτέροισιν ὀνείδεα μυθήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.246; ἔστι μὲν εὕδειν, ἔστι δὲ τερπομένοισιν ἀκούειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.392}}; εἴ τί πού ἐστι (i.e. πιθέσθαι){{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.193; τοιάδε.. ἐστὶν ἀκοῦσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1055 (_anapaest meter_); ἔστι τεκμήρια ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1163, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.105, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.6, etc.}}; so in _imperative_, _optative_, and _subjunctive_, ἔστω ἀποφέρεσθαι τῷ βουλομένῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.7; μυρία ἂν εἴη λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; ὅπως ἂν ᾖ δρᾶν “IG” 2.1054.91: more frequently in _negative_ clauses, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.267, etc.}}; followed by ὥστε with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 656}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἁδόντα δ᾽ εἴη με τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ὁμιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.96; ἔστιν ἐκπεσεῖν ἀρχῆς Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 757}}: sometimes not _impersonal_ in this sense, θάλασσα δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἦν ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 419.}}
**A.VI.b** ἔστω in argument, **let it be granted**, ἔστω τοῦτο ἀληθὲς εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; ἔστω σοι τοῦθ᾽ οὕτως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.987b; ἔστω εἶναί τινα τοιοῦτον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr.74.24.}}
**B** most frequently, **to be**, the Copula connecting the predicate with the Subject, both being in the same case: hence, **signify, import**, τὸ γὰρ εἴρειν λέγειν ἐστίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398d}}; especially in the phrase τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι, **hoc est**; Σκαιόλαν, ὅπερ ἐστὶ Λαϊόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 17: with numerals, τὰ δὶς πέντε δέκα ἐστίν}} twice five **are** ten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.7}}; εἶναί τις or τι, **to be** somebody, something, **be** of some consequence, see at {τι; οὐδὲν εἶναι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.}}
**B.2** _periphrastic_ with the Participle to represent the finite Verb: with _perfect_ _participle_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τετληότες εἰμέν, for τετλήκαμεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.873}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἦν τεθνηκώς, for ἐτεθνήκει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 869}}; ἔσται δεδορκώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1179; εἰμὶ γεγώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1299; πεφυκός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1473; δεδρακότες εἰσίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68; κατακεκονότες ἔσεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.36}}: with _aorist_ _participle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βλήμενος ἦν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.211}}; so προδείσας εἰμί, οὐ σιωπήσας ἔσε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 90, 1146, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 460}}: with _present_ _participle_, ἦν προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 371; φεύγων Ὀρέστης ἐστίν [prev. author] “Ch.” 136; εἴην οὐκ ἂν εὖ φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1330}}; τί δ᾽ ἐστί.. φέρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 991, compare 274, 708; λέγων ἐστίν τις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1179; ἦν τίς σ᾽ ὑβρίζων [prev. author] “HF” 313; πόρρω ἤδη εἶ πορευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204b; βαδίζων εἰμί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 36; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἦσαν ἱέντες 1.57, +others; even εἰσὶ διάφοροι ἐόντες 3.49 (assuming variant):}} — if the _Article_ is joined with the Part., the noun is made emphatic, Κᾶρές εἰσι οἱ καταδέξαντες the persons who showed her **were** Carians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.171; αὐτὸς ἦν ὁ μαρτυρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798; δόλος ἦν ὁ φράσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 197 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**C** εἶναι is frequently modified in sense by the addition of _adverbs_, or the cases of Nouns without or with Preps.:
**C.I** εἶναι with _adverbs_, where the _adverb_ often merely represents a Noun and stands as the predicate, ἅλις δέ οἱ ἦσαν ἄρουραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.122, etc.}}; ἀκέων, ἀκήν εἶναι, **to be** silent,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.82; σῖγα πᾶς ἔστω λεώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 532; διαγνῶναι χαλεπῶς ἦν ἄνδρα ἕκαστον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.424}}; ἀσφαλέως ἡ κομιδὴ ἔσται **will go on** safely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}; ἐγγύς, πόρρω εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e: frequently _impersonal_ with words implying good or ill fortune, Κουρήτεσσι κακῶς ἦν}} **it fared** ill with them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.551; εὖ γὰρ ἔσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1188, etc.; ἡδέως ἂν αὐτοῖς εἴη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.30.}}
**C.II** with _genitive_, to express descent or extraction, πατρὸς δ᾽ εἴμ᾽ ἀγαθοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.109; αἵματός εἰς ἀγαθοῖο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71, etc.; πόλεως μεγίστης εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.19.}}
**C.II.b** to express the material of which a thing is made, ἡ κρηπίς ἐστι λίθων μεγάλων **consists of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93; τῆς πόλιος ἐούσης δύο φαρσέων [prev. work] 186}}; τοιούτων ἔργων ἐστὶ ἡ τυραννίς **is made up of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.ή, etc.
**C.II.c** to express the class to which a person or thing belongs, εἶ γὰρ τῶν φίλων **you are one of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 345; ἐτύγχανε βουλῆς ὤν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; ὅσοι ἦσαν τῶν προτέρων στρατιωτῶν [prev. author] 7.44; Κριτίας τῶν τριάκοντα ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31}}; ἔστι τῶν αἰσχρῶν **it is in the class of** disgraceful things, i. e. **it is** disgraceful, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2.}}
**C.II.d** to express that a thing belongs to another, Τροίαν Ἀχαιῶν οὖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 269; τὸ πεδίον ἦν μέν κοτε Χορασμίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, etc.}}: hence, **to be of the party of**, ἦσαν.. τινὲς μὲν φιλίππου, τινὲς δὲ τοῦ βελτίστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.56, compare 37.53}}; **to be de pendent upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 737, etc.}}; **to be at the mercy of**, ἔστι τοῦ λέγοντος, ἢν φόβους λέγῃ {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 917.}}
**C.II.e** to express one's duty, business, custom, nature, and the like, οὔτοι γυναικός ἐστι **'tis** not a woman's **part**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 940; τὸ ἐπιτιμᾶν παντὸς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16}}; τὸ δὲ ναυτικὸν τέχνης ἐστίν **is matter of** art, **requires** art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 83.}}
**C.II.f** in LXX, **to be occupied about**, ἦσαν τοῦ θύειν {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.30.17}}; ἔσεσθαι, with _genitive_, **to be about to**, ἐσόμεθα τοῦ σῶσαί σε {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.10.11.}}
**C.III** with the _dative_, ἐστί μοι I **have**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}
**C.III.2** with two datives., σφίσι τε καὶ Ἀθηναίοισι εἶναι οὐδὲν πρῆγμα that they and the Athenians **have** nothing **to do** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84; μηδὲν εἶναι σοὶ καὶ φιλίππῳ πρᾶγμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; more shortly, σοί τε καὶ τούτοισι πρήγμασι τί ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33}}; τί τῷ νόμῳ καὶ τῇ βασάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.36}}; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σο; Latin _quid tecum est mihi?_ {{NT|NT.Mark.5.7, etc.}}; also ἐμοὶ οὐδὲν πρὸς τοὺς τοιούτους (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.12; ἐν οἷς πρὸς τοὺς ἐναντίους ἐστὶ τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.278}}; ἔσται αὐτῳ πρὸς τὸν θεόν, in tomb inscriptions, {{Refs|“JHS” 18.113, etc.}}
**C.III.3** with ἄσμενος, βουλόμενος, etc., added, ἐμοὶ δέ κεν ἀσμένῳ εἴη **'twould be** to my delight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.108; οὐκ ἂν σφίσι βουλομένοις εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35; προσδεχομένῳ [prev. author] 6.46; θέλοντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1356 (Lyric poetry); ἡδομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187c.}}
**C.IV** with Preps., εἶναι ἀπό τινος, ={εἶναί τινος}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(above 11.a), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.9; εἰσὶν ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίων αὗται πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12d}}; but εἶναι ἀπ᾽ οἴκου **to be** away from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99.}}
**C.IV.2** εἶναι ἔκ τινος **to be sprung** from, εἴμ᾽ ἐκ Παιονίης, Μυρμιδόνων ἔξ εἰμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.154, 24.397, etc.}}; ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης **it is** of necessity, i. e. necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 256d.}}
**C.IV.3** εἶναι ἐν.. **to be** in a certain state, ἐν εὐπαθείῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; ἐν ἀθυμία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, etc.; ἐν ταραχαῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218}}; εἶναι ἐν ἀξιώματι **to be** in esteem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130}}; οἱ ἐν τέλεϊ ἐόντες those in office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.18, etc.}}; but εἶναι ἐν τέχνῃ, ἐν φιλοσοφία **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a.}}
**C.IV.3.b** ἐν σοί ἐστι **it depends** on thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 963; ἐν σοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν [prev. author] “OT” 314; so also ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Ph.” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2, etc.}}
**C.IV.4** εἶναι διά.., much like{εἶναι ἐν}.., εἶναι διὰ φόβου, ={φοβεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}; εἶναι δι᾽ ὄχλου, ={ὀχληρὸν εἶναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.73; εἶναι διὰ μόχθων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.25}}; εἶναι δι᾽ αἰτίας, ={αἰτιᾶσθαι}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.70}}; Geometry texts, **pass** through, διὰ τᾶς ἑτέρας διαμέτρου ἐόντος τοῦ ἐπιπέδου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 20.}}
**C.IV.5** εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῆς **to be** by oneself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.23}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ ὀνόματος **to bear** a name, {{Refs|[prev. author] 39.21}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τοῖς πράγμασιν **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.12}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινα **to be** against him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.33}}; εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑξήκοντα στάδια **to reach** sixty stadia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τὰς ἁφάς **pass** through the points of contact, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 4.1}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινι, see above 3 b.
**C.IV.6** εἶναι πρός τινος **to be** in one's favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10, 29, etc.}}; **to suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; εἶναι πρός τινι **engaged** in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.31}}; πρὸς τοῖς ἰδίοις **mind** one's own affairs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a6, “Ath.” 16.3; εἶναι πρὸς τὸ κωλύειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.3}}; πρὸς τὸ πονεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Teles p.46 H.; εἶναι περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7, etc.}}
**C.IV.7** εἶναι παρά τινι or τινα, = παρειναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.2.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά}} (assuming variant).
**C.IV.8** εἶναι ὑπό τινα or τινι **to be** subject to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.17 (assuming variant), 6.2.4.}}
**C.IV.9** περὶ τούτων ἐστίν that **is** the question, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 30.}}
**C.IV.10** εἶναι ἀπό.., in Geometry texts, **to be constructed** upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.9, “Con.Sph.” 7.}}
**D** ἐστί is very frequently omitted, mostly in the _present_ _indicative_ before certain predicates, as ἀνάγκη, ἄξιον, δυνατόν, εἰκός, ἕτοιμον, οἷόν τε, ῥᾴδιον, χρεών, etc., and after the _neuter_ of Verbals in -τέος, and such forms as θαυμαστὸν ὅσον: less frequently with other persons and moods, εἰμί omitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 92, “Aj.” 813}}; εἶ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.206}}; ἐσμέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634}}; ἐστέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.463}}; εἰσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 499 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 2.778 B}}; _subjunctive_ ᾖ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.376, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 659, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32}}; _optative_ εἴη, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1183.12}}; _imperfect_ ἦν, {{Refs|[prev. work]2.778 B}}; _future_ ἔσονται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.394.}}
**E** the Inf. frequently seems redundant,
**E.1** in phrases implying power or will to do a thing, ἑκὼν εἶναι (see. ἑκών) κατὰ δύναμιν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.32; εἰς δύναμιν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300c}}; τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐκείνοις εἶναι, **quantum in illis esset**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.58; τὸ ἐπὶ σφᾶς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; τὸ κατὰ τοῦτον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9; κατὰ τοῦτο εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a}}; τὸ τήμερον, τὸ νῦν εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 396e, “La.” 201c, Theopomp. Comedy texts 98, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2, etc.}}
**E.2** after Verbs of naming or choosing, σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσι τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e; σύμμαχόν μιν εἵλοντο εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.134}}; of giving, δῶκε ξεινήϊον εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.20.}}
**F** _imperfect_ ἦνissts. used where other languages take the _present_,
**F.1** after ἄρα, to express a fact which **is and has always been** the same, δέρμα δὲ ἀνθρώπου.. ἦν ἄρα σχεδὸν δερμάτων πάντων λαμπρότατον human skin then **it appears is**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; Κύπρις οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἦν θεός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 359}}; ὡς ἄρ᾽ ἦσθ᾽ ἐμὸς πατὴρ ὀρθῶς{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1169; ἦ πολύμοχθον ἄρ᾽ ἦν γένος.. ἁμερίων [prev. author] “IA” 1330; ἦ στωμύλος ἦσθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.79}}; so also when there is reference to a past thought, τουτὶ τί ἦ; what **is** this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 157, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 387c: so in the Aristotelian formula τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (“APo.” 82b38, +others)}}, used to express **the essential nature** of a thing, where τί ἦν (for ἐστί) takes the place of the _dative_ in such phrases as τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, τὸ μεγέθει εἶναι, {{Refs|“APr.” 67b12, “de An.” 429b10.}}
**G** ἐγώ εἰμι, in {{LXX|LXX, pleonastic for ἐγώ, ἐγώ εἰμι οὐχ ἥμαρτον LXX.Judg.11.27, compare 6.18; also ἔσται πᾶς ἀποκτενεῖ με LXX.Gen.4.14.}}

5608:
** ὠνέομαι**,
{{Refs|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 341, etc.}}: _future_ **-ήσομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 360, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 815, “Pax” 1261, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.22, _Doric dialect_ ὠνασοῦμαι (see. below)}}:—in _Attic dialect_ usually with the syllabic augment, ἐωνούμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 184, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.134, ἀντ-εωνεῖτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.26, etc.: but ὠνέετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.139, ὠνέοντο [prev. author] 1.69, ὠνούμην 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.4 codices, ἀντ-ωνεῖτο 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.134, ἐξ-ωνεῖτο 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.91}}: _aorist 1_ ἐωνησάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 3; ὠνησάμην 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 10, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 81}}; _participle_ ὠνησάμενος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.50.3, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.20: ὠνήσασθαι}} not in Attic _inscriptions_ earlier than {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 22.1035.8 (1st c.BC), ἐπριάμην}} being used in _Attic dialect_; ὠνησάμην in the proverbial Χῖος δεσπότην ὠνήσατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 269:}}_perfect_ ἐώνημαι in _active_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 7, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.2}} (so _pluperfect_ ἐώνητο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.5)}}; also as _passive_ (see. below 11): _aorist_ in _passive_ sense (see. below 11) ἐωνήθη; _future_ in _passive_ sense ἀπ-ωνηθήσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 84: this verb is usually replaced in later Gr. by ἀγοράζω}}:—**buy, purchase,** opposed to πωλέω, πιπράσκ; πῶ τις ὦν ὄνον ὠνασεῖτα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 125}}; but in _present_ and _imperfect_ (which are the tenses most in use), **offer to buy, bargain** or **bid** for a thing, ὄφρ᾽ ἄλλων ὠνῇ κλῆρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 341}}; ὠνέεσθαι τῶν φορτίων **wished to buy** some of their wares, **began to bargain** for them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1}}; Κροῖσός σφι ὠνεομένοισι ἔδωκε gave it them **when they offered to buy,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 69; τὰς νήσους οὐκ ἐβούλοντο ὠνευμένοισι πωλέειν [prev. work] 165, compare 3.139, 6.121}}; ὀκτὼ λάβοις ἄν (i.e. ὀβολούς); Answ. εἴπερ ὠνεῖ τὸν ἕτερον if **you are willing to buy** the other fish, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 16.10, compare 78.7; ὠνεῖσθαι καὶ πωλεῖν πρὸς ἀλλήλους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 741b; ὠ. τὰς γυναῖκας παρὰ τῶν γονέων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313d, 313e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.48; ἀπό τινος 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 5.17}}: with _dative_ _person_, **buy from**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 815, “Pax” 1261}}; also ὠ. ἐκ Κορίνθου **buy goods** from Corinth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.17: ὠ. ἐξ ἀγορᾶς [prev. author] “An.” 3.2.21}}; _metaphorically_, καιρόν, σπονδάς ὠ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 6, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.7.9; ὠ. μὴ ἀδικεῖσθαι τοὺς ἐμπόρους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.25}}; with _genitive_ pretii, **buy for** so much, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.6, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 360, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.24}}; ψυχῆς **at the price of** life, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 85}}: also with _dative_, **buy with**.., τἄχθιστα τοῖσι φιλτάτοις ὠνούμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1170: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.10.4, “Ages.” 1.18: especially in participle, ὠνουμένους ἕξειν τὰ ἐπιτήδεια}}**by purchase,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 2.3.27, compare 5.5.14, etc.}}; also ὁ ὠνούμενος the **buyer, purchaser,** ὁρῶντος τοῦ ὠνουμένου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 3.2, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 36}}; ὁ ἐωνημένος the **owner by purchase** (of a slave), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 7; ὁ ὠνησάμενος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.242d}}; ὁ ὠνησόμενος the **intending purchaser,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.10: _metaphorically_, χάριτας πονηρὰς ὠ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 902; ὅσα ἄνθρωποι ἄθλων ὠνοῦνται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.11; εὔνοιαν παρά τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.20; ὠ. τὰς αὑτῶν ψυχὰς παρὰ τῶν ἐχθρῶν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.9:—in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 337 Robortello restored ὄνοιτο.}}
**2** **bid for, purchase the farming of public taxes** or **properties,** λ ταλάντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.134, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.2 (in _participle_ _perfect_ _passive_ with _transitive_ sense)}}; τέλη παρὰ τῆς πόλεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.19, etc.; ὠ. μέταλλα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.293; τὸν ἐωνημένον τὴν ἰλὺν ἐκκομίσασθαι “IG” 12.94.20, compare ὠνή 11.}}
**3** **buy off, avert by giving hush-money,** ὠ. τὸν κίνδυνον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.20}}; τὰ ἐγκλήματα {{Refs|[prev. work]8}}; ταλάντου τὸ πλημμέλημα (i.e. its penalty) παρά τινος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 81.}}
**4** ὠ. τινα **to buy** a person, of one who bribes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.247; ὠνεῖται καὶ διαφθείρει τινάς [prev. author] 9.45, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 15.}}
**II** sometimes used as _passive_, uncertain in _present_ since [ὠνούμενά τε καὶ πιπρασκόμενα] is interpolated in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69b}}; occasionally in _perfect_, _participle_ ἐωνημένος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 563b, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.42, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.209}} (but _indicative_ ἐώνηνται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Anonymus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1410a19 is _active_ in sense)}}: _pluperfect_ ἐώνητο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1182 (troch.)}}; also in _aorist_ ἐωνήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.12, ὠνηθῇ [prev. author] “Vect.” 4.19}}; _participle_ ὠνηθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 224a, “Lg.” 850a.}}
**III** _active_ _perfect_ _participle_ ἐωνηκώς, ={ἐωνημένος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 135S.:}}_aorist_ ὠνῆσαι· ἀγοράσαι, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Zonaras Legal icographus:}}_present_ ὠνεῖν· πωλεῖν, ἀπολαύειν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus: the sense πωλεῖν is Cretan, ὠνῆν τὰ χρήματα}} they shall **sell** the property, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 5.47}}; αἰ δέ τις.. τὸ νόμισμα μὴ λείοι δέκετθαι ἢ καρπῶ ὠνίοι if any one refuses the currency or **sells** for produce, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 525.8 (Crete, 3rd c.BC).}}

5609:
** ᾠόν**, τό,
old _poetry_ forms **ὤεον**, **ὤιον**, see entry near the end:— **egg,** τίκτει ᾠὰ ἐν γῇ καὶ ἐκλέπει [ὁ κροκόδειλος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.68; ᾠὰ χήνεια 4th c.BC: Eriphus Comicus 7}}; of all birds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 559a15}}; but mostly of **hens' eggs,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 856, “fragment” 185, etc.}}; [ᾠοῦ] τὸ λευκόν, τὸ ὠχρόν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 559a18}}; τὸ πυρρόν, τὸ χρυσοῦν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.171, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.376d}}; ᾠὰ ἡμιπαγέα half-boiled **eggs,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”)}}; ἑφθά, ὠμά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Vertigine” 2; ᾠὸν ῥοφεῖν 3rd c.BC: Nicomachus Comicus 3; καταπίνειν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 140.5; ᾠὰ κολάπτειν 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 18.4 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ᾠὰ γόνιμα fertile **eggs,** opposed to ὑπηνέμια, ἄγονα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 730a6, 20; also ᾠὰ πλήρη [prev. author] “Mete.” 359a14}}; ᾠὸν τέλειον, opposed to ἀτελές, {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 718b23, 24}}; ᾠὸν ἀνεμιαῖον, ζεφύριον, wind-**egg,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Araros Comicus 6, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 560a6}}; σμύρνης ᾠ. **lump,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.73; ὁ Χρόνος ἐγέννησεν ᾠόν 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 54, +others, compare 6th c.BC: Epimenides Philosophus 5: _metaphorically_, ᾠὸν ἅπας γέγονεν}} he has become **bald as an egg,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 11.398 (from Nicias).}}
**2** of the **eggs** or **spawn** of fish, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93; τὰ ᾠὰ ἀφιᾶσι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 567b22, compare 525a7}}; of serpents, {{Refs|[prev. work] 558a26}}; of tortoises, {{Refs|[prev. work] 558a4.}}
**3** of plants, **seed,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 731a6; compare ᾠοτοκέω 2.}}
**4** **cupping-glass,** τὰ ἰατρικὰ ᾠὰ ὑέλινα ὄντακαὶ σύστομα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 1}}**Prooem.: egg-shaped cup,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dinon Historicus 14}}. The word has the following forms: _Attic dialect_ **ᾠόν** (- ?~X), confirmed by Inscrr. ὠιῶν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(2).224 A 11,12 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}, Papyri (ὠιὰ χήνεα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 130.26 (3rd c.BC))}}, and later Mss.; _Aeolic dialect_ **ὤιον**, _genitive_ ὠίω (¯ ?~X ?~X ¯), {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 56, 112}}; **ὤεον** lbyc.16, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 11, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 6.10, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 192, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 956}}; **ὤβεον** is Argive _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (i.e. ὤϝεον)}}; ᾠόν _oxytone_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Theognostus Grammaticus “Can.” 130; ὤεον _proparoxytone_, [prev. work]121. The form ὠόν}} which Latin _ovum_ would lead us to expect, is found only in late texts {{LXX+NT|(LXX.Deu.22.6, NT.Luke.11.12, etc.)}} and is due to loss of the **ι** in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC}}; _cognate_ with Latin _ovum,_ OHG**ei,** ONorse **egg** (prim. Germanic _aiia -_), whence Engl. **examples**

5610:
** ὥρα**,
**C**_Ionic dialect_ **ὥρη**, ἡ: _Epic dialect_ _genitive_ _plural_ ὡράων, _Ionic dialect_ ὡρέων: _locative_ _plural_ ὥρασι, (which see) **any period,** fixed by natural laws and revolutions, whether of the year, month, or day (the sense 'day' is implied in the _compound_ ἑπτάωρος, which see), νυκτός τε ὥραν καὶ μηνὸς καὶ ἐνιαυτοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.7.4, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 449 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 527d; τοῦ γνώμονος ἡ σκιὰ ἐπιοῦσα ἐπὶ τὰς γραμμὰς σημαίνει τὰς ὥρας τοῦ ἐνιαυτοῦ καὶ τῆς ἡμέρας “IG” 12(8).240 (from Samothrace): but specially}}
**C.I** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **part of the year, season;** mostly in _plural_, the **seasons,** ὅτε τέτρατον ἦλθεν ἔτος καὶ ἐπήλυθον ὧραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.107, 19.152; ἀλλ᾽ ὅτε δὴ μῆνές τε καὶ ἡμέραι ἐξετελεῦντο, ἂψ περιτελλομένου ἔτεος, καὶ ἐπήλυθον ὧραι 11.295, 14.294; ἀλλ᾽ ὅτε δή ῥ᾽ ἐνιαυτὸς ἔην, περὶ δ᾽ ἔτραπον ὧραι 10.469, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 58; Διὸς ὧραι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.344, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.2; ὁ κύκλος τῶν ὡρέων ἐς τὠυτὸ περιιών 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.4, compare 1.32; δυώδεκα μέρεα δασαμένους τῶν ὡρέων ἐς [τὸν ἐνιαυτόν] [prev. author] 2.4}}; οὐ μεταλλάσσουσι αἱ ὧραι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 77; περιτελλομέναις ὥραις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 156 (Lyric poetry)}}; πάσαις ὥραις at all **seasons,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 592.6 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 696 (_anapaest meter_); ὧραι ἐτῶν καὶ ἐνιαυτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 906c, compare “Smp.” 188a, etc.; τῆς.. ὥρας τοῦ ἐνιαυτοῦ ταύτης οὔσης, ἐν ᾗ ἀσθενοῦσιν ἄνθρωποι μάλιστα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.47}}; χαλεπὴ ὥ. a bad **season,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 344d; ἀ δ᾽ ὤρα χαλέπα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 39}}; ἡ ὥ. αὕτη this **season,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 7.1, compare 5.6}}; κατὰ τὰς ὥρας according to the **seasons,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 786a31; οἱ περὶ τὴν ὥραν χρόνοι [prev. author] “Pol.” 1335a37.— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus distinguish three seasons, and express each by the _sing._ ὥρη, with a word added to specify each:}}
**C.I.a** **spring,** ἔαρος.. ὥρη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.148; ὥρη εἰαρινή 2.471, 16.643, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.367, etc.}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἦρος ὥρα or ὧραι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1008 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 508 (Lyric poetry); ὥρα νέα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 419; νεᾶνις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 786 (Lyric poetry); see below 2.}}
**C.I.b** **summer,** θέρεος ὥρη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 584, 664; ὥρα θερινή 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 987a, etc.}}
**C.I.c** **winter,** χείματος ὥρη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 450; ὥρῃ χειμερίῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.485, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 494}}; χειμῶνος ὥρᾳ in winter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.137; χιονοβόλος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.182e.— 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} also names three seasons, {{Refs|“Pr.” 454s q.}}; an Egyptian division of the year, _accusative_ to {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.26.}}—A fourth first appears in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 76}}, θέρος καὶ χεῖμα κὠπώραν τρίταν καὶ τέτρατον τὸ ϝῆ; and in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 3.68, χειμών, ἦρ, θέρος, φθινόπωρο; ὥρας φαίνομεν ἡμεῖς ἦρος χειμῶνος ὀπώρας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 709 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τετράμορφοι ὧραι E(?).{{Refs|“fragment” 943 (hexameters)}}: later, seven seasons are named, ἔαρ, θέρος, ὀπώρα, φθινόπωρον, σπορητός, χειμών, φυταλιά {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).17.}}
**C.I.2** especially **prime of the year, springtime,** ὅσα φύλλα καὶ ἄνθεα γίγνεται ὥρῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.51, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.468; παρὰ τὴν καθεστηκυῖαν ὥραν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.6.}}
**C.I.2.b** in historians, **the campaigning season,** τὸν τῆς ὥρας εἰς τὸν περίπλουν χρόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.13}}; especially in the phrase ὥρα ἔτους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.52, 6.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229a, “Lg.” 952e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.23, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.23.2}}; εἰς ἔτους ὥραν next **season,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 10.}}
**C.I.3** **the year** generally, τῆς ὥρης μέσον θέρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.12}}; ἐν τῇ πέρυσιν ὥρᾳ last **year,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.3}}; εἰς ὥρας next **year,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 116, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 346c, LXX.Gen.18.10, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.17 (1st c.AD: Nicarchus Epigrammaticus), compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 22; also εἰς ἄλλας ὥρας}}**hereafter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 122 (Lyric poetry); ἐς τὰς ὥρας τὰς ἑτέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 562 (Lyric poetry); ἐκ τῶν ὡρῶν εἰς τὰς ὥρας [prev. author] “Th.” 950 (_anapaest meter_)}}; κἠς ὥρας κἤπειτα **next year** and for ever, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.74; also ὥραις ἐξ ὡρᾶν 3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 25}}; compare ὥρασιν.
**C.I.4** in _plural_, of **the climate** of a country, **as determined by its seasons,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.142, compare 149, 4.199}} (here perhaps three **harvest seasons**); τὰς ὥ. κάλλιστα κεκρημένας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.106; compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 111e, “Phd.” 111b}}; **climatic conditions,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.26.}}
**C.II** **time of day,** νυκτὸς ἐν ὥρῃ {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 67, 155, 400}}; αἱ ὧ. τῆς ἡμέρας the **times** of day, i.e. morning, noon, evening, and night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.4}}; δι᾽ ὥραν ἡμέρας by **the time** of day (fixed for meetings), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 49, etc.; πᾶσαν ὥ. τῆς ἡμέρας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 371b31; μεσονυκτίοις ποθ᾽ ὥραις “Anacreontea” 31.1: without ἡμέρας}} or νυκτός, ἑκάστης ἡμέρας μέχρι τρίτου μέρους ὥρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 784a; τῆς ὥρας μικρὸν πρὸ δύντος ἡλίου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.22}}; ψευσθεὶς τῆς ὥ. having mistaken the **hour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.38}}; ἐποίησαν ἔξω μέσων νυκτῶν τὴν ὥραν, i.e. they prolonged the **day** beyond midnight, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.26; τῆς ὥρας ἐγίγνετ᾽ ὀψέ [prev. author] 21.84; ὀψίτερον τῆς ὥ. “PTeb.” 793 xi 12 (2nd c.BC)}}; πολλῆς ὥρας **it being late,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.8.3; ἤδη ὥρα πολλή NT.Mark.6.35}}; ἄχρι πολλῆς ὥρας till **late in the day,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.54.}}
**C.II.b** **duration, interval** or **lapse of time,** μετὰ ἱκανὴν ὥραν τοῦ κατενεχθῆναι τὸν πέλεκυν ἐξακούεται ἡ τῆς πληγῆς φωνή {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.69}}; **length of time, term,** Ἄρτεμις ἐννέ᾽ ἐτῶν δεκάδας βίον Ἀρτεμιδώρῳ ἔκχρησεν, τρεῖς δ᾽ ὥραι(date.) ἔτι προσέθηκε Προνοίη {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12(3).1350.3 (Thera, 2nd c.BC)}}; ἐπὶ πολλὴν ὥ. for a long **time,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.4.4.}}
**C.II.2** the νυχθήμερον was probably first divided into twenty-four **hours** by {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus, ἐν πόσαις ὥραις ἰσημεριναῖς}} (equinoctial **hours**) ἕκαστον τῶν ἄστρων ἢ δύνει ἢ ἀνατέλλει {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2.4.5, compare 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 3.9, 4.9, +others}}
**C.II.2.b** in ordinary life the day from sunrise to sunset was divided into twelve equal parts called ὧραι (ὧραι καιρικαί when it was necessary to distinguish them from the ὧραι ἰσημεριναί, see at {καιρικός} 2 c), ἡμέρα ἡ.. δωδεκάωρος, τουτέστιν ἡ ἀπὸ ἀνατολῆς μέχρι δύσεως {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.182; οὐχὶ δώδεκά εἰσιν ὧραι τῆς ἡμέρα; NT.John.11.9; ὡράων ἀμφὶ δυωδεκάδι “Anthologia Graeca” 9.782 (6th c.AD: Paulus Silentiarius Epigrammaticus)}}; the time of day was commonly given without the _Article_, ὥρᾳ ᾱ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PHamb.” 1.96.3 (2nd c.AD)}}, τρίτης ὥρας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 12}}; ὀγδόης, ἐνάτης, δεκάτης ὥ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Alex.” 60, “Aem.” 22, “Ant.” 68, etc.}}; but we have περὶ τὴν τρίτην ὥραν, περὶ τὴν ἑνδεκάτην, {{NT+1st c.BC+|NT.Matt.20.3, 6, beside περὶ ἕκτην καὶ ἐννάτην ὥ. [prev. work] 5; χθὲς ὥραν ἑβδόμην NT.John.4.52, compare “IG” 5(1).1390.109 (Andania, 1st c.BC), etc.}}; ἐρωτᾷ σε Χαιρήμων δειπνῆσαι.. αὔριον, ἥτις ἐστὶν ιε, ἀπὸ ὥρας θ ¯ -to-morrow the 15th at 9 **o'clock,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 110 (2nd c.AD): proverbial, δωδεκάτης ὥ.}} as we say 'at the eleventh **hour',** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 17.}}
**C.II.2.c** τὰ δυώδεκα μέρεα τῆς ἡμέρης παρὰ Βαβυλωνίων ἔμαθον οἱ Ἕλληνες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.109; here ἡμέρη means the νυχθήμερον, and the μέρεα were each = 2}} ὧραι ἰσημερινα; these **double hours** (Assyr. **kaš-bu**) are called ὧραι by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus, ἥμισυ ζῳδίου.., ὅ ἐστιν ὥρας ἥμισυ “Ars” 14.11, compare 16.2; compare δωδεκάωρος 11.}}
**C.III** Astrology texts, **degree** of the zodiac rising at the nativity compare (ὡρονόμος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|11, ὡροσκόπος 11), ὥ. μεροποσπόρος, τεκνοσπόρος, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 4.577, 597}}; ἐξ ὥρης ἐσορῶν Ζεὺς Ἑρμείην Jupiter in the **ascendant** in aspect with Mercury, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.186, compare 32, +others}}
**B** **the fitting time** or **season** for a thing (mostly without _Article_, even in _Attic dialect_), frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below); ὥρα συνάπτει 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.247; ὧραι ἐπειγόμεναι [prev. author] “N.” 4.34; ὅταν ὥ. ἥκῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.2}}; but with _Article_, τῆς ὥ. ἐνθυμεῖσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 8.6}}: frequently in later writers, τῆς ὥρας ἐπιγενομένης {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.34.3, etc.}}
**B.2** with _genitive_ _of things_, ὥρη κοίτοιο, μύθων, ὕπνου, **the time** for bed, tale-telling, or sleep, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.334, 11.379, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10; ὥρη δόρποιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.407; περὶ ἀρίστου ὥραν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.81, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.13; πολυηράτου ἐς γάμου ὥρην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.126; ἐς γάμου ὥρην ἀπικέσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.61; γάμων ἔχειν ὥραν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.32}}; so εἰς ἀνδρὸς ὥραν ἥκουσα **time** for a husband, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 113d}}; ὥρη ἀρότου, ἀμήτου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 460, 575; μέχρι ἀρότου ὥρης “IG” 7.235.3 (Oropus, 4th c.BC)}}; καρπῶν ὧραι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1034 (_anapaest meter_); ἡ ὥρα τῆς ὀχείας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 509b20; τοῦ φωλεύειν [prev. work] 579a26, etc.}}; also ὥραν εἶχον παιδεύεσθαι I was of **age** to.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.28.}}
**B.3** ὥρα [ἐστίν] with _infinitive_, **it is time** to do a thing, ἀλλὰ καὶ ὥρη εὕδειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.330, compare 373}}; so also in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1584, “Heracl.” 288 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 361e, 362a; so δοκεῖ οὐχ ὥρα εἶναι καθεύδειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.11, compare “HG” 7.2.13 (uncertain reading)}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ὥρα δ᾽ ἐμπόρους καθιέναι ἄγκυραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 661, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 466 (Lyric poetry)}}: with _dative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 145b}}: in these phrases the _infinitive_ _present_ is almost universal; the _aorist_, however, occurs in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.428, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 245 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 393 (where also ἐστί is added to ὥρα, as in 5th c.BC: Philyllius Comicus 3, ἀφαιρεῖν ὥρα 'στὶν ἤδη τὰς τραπέζας)}}; and the _perfect_ in ὥρα πεπαῦσθαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.728d}}: sometimes the _infinitive_ must be supplied, οὐδέ τί σε χρή, πρὶν ὥρη, καταλέχθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.394, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 112 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 877}}; ὥρα κἠς οἶκον (i. e. ἰέναι εἰς οἶκον) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.147.}}
**B.4** in various _adverbial_ usages, τὴν ὥρην **at the right time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, 8.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.16: but τὴν ὥ.}} **at** that **hour,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 401}}; ταύτην τὴν ὥραν **at** this **season,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.1; [ἡ ἶρις] πᾶσαν ὥραν γίγνεται τῆς ἡμέρας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 371b31; δείελον ὥρην παύομαι ἀμήτοιο 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.417}}; ὥραν οὐδενὸς κοινὴν θεῶν **at an hour**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 109, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 724, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.9}}; αὐτῆς ὥρας immediately, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PMich.” in “Class.Phil.” 22.255(3rd c.AD)}}; ἐν ὥρῃ in **due season,** in **good time,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.176, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 242, etc.}}; also αἰεὶ εἰς ὥρας in successive **seasons,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.135}}; ἐς τὰς ὥρας for **all time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 382 (Lyric poetry compare above 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.3) (hence in an acclamation [ε] ἰς ὥρας πᾶσι τοῖς τὴν πόλιν φιλοῦσιν}} **hurrah** for.., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 41.29 (3rd-4th c.AD))}}; οἱ ὧδε χέζοντες εἰς ὥ. μὴ ἔλθοιεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Milet.” 2(3) No.406, compare ὥρασ; καθ᾽ ὥραν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.12, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.45.4, compare 3.93.6, etc.; opposed to παρ᾽ ὥρην “Anthologia Graeca” 7.534 (3rd c.BC: Alexander Aetolus Elegiacus or 1st c.AD: Automedon Epigrammaticus), compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.784b, etc.:— πρὸ τῆς ὥρας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.16; πρὸ ὥρας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Luct.” 13; πρὸ ὥρας τελευτῆσαι “IG” 42(1).84.26 (Epid., 1st c.AD)}}; πρὶν ὥρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.43 (compare πρίν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.4).}}
**B.II** _metaphorically_, **the spring-time of life, the bloom of youth,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 3.1; ὥραν ἐχούσας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 997, compare “Th.” 13, 535; παῖδας πρὸς τέρμασιν ὥρας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 705 (_anapaest meter_); πάντες οἱ ἐν ὥρᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474d}}; οὐκ ἐνὥ., ={πρεσβύτερος}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 240d; ἐὰν ἐπὶ ὥρᾳ ᾖ [prev. author] “R.” 474e; ἕως ἂν ἐν ὥρᾳ ὦσι [prev. author] “Men.” 76b}}; παυσαμένου τῆς ὥ. probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 234a; ἀνθεῖν ἐν ὥ. [prev. author] “R.” 475a; τὴν ὥ. διαφυλάξαι ἄβατον τοῖς πονηροῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.58}}; λήγειν ὥρας, opposed to ἀνθεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.131e; ἑς ἐπιγινόμενόν τι τέλος, οἷον τοῖς ἀκμαίοις ἡ ὥρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1174b33, compare 1157a8.}}
**B.II.2** frequently involving an idea of **beauty,** φεῦ φεῦ τῆς ὥρας τοῦ κάλλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1724 (Lyric poetry); ὥρᾳ.. ἡλικίας λαμπρός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54; κάλλει καὶ ὥρᾳ διενεγκόντες 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.134, compare [prev. work]158; καλὸς ὥρᾳ τε κεκραμένος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).104, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 837b}}; ἀφ᾽ ὥρας ἐργάζεσθαι **quaestum corpore facere,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus}}“Tim..” 14,compare {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus}}“Mem..” 1.6.13, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Smp.” 8.21; τὴν ὥ. πεπωληκότες 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.344 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus:}} —then,
**B.II.2.b** generally, **beauty, grace, elegance** of style, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.874b, etc.; γλυκύτης καὶ ὥ. 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.3, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Rh.” p.335 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 1.2}}; of **beauty** in general, χάρις καὶ ὥρα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.128d.}}
**B.II.3** Ὥρα personified, like{Ἥβη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.1.}}
**B.III** ={τὰ ὡραῖα}, the **produce of the season, fruits of the year,** ἀπὸ τῆς ὥρας ἐτρέφοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.1.}}
**C** personified, αἱὯραι, the **Hours,** keepers of heaven's cloudgate, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.749, 8.393}}; and ministers of the gods,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 433; Ζεῦ, τεαὶ.. Ὧραι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.2}}; especially of Aphrodite, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 6.5,12}}; also Ὧ. Διονυσιάδες, Καρνειάδες, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 148, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 87}}; three in number, Eunomia, Dike, Eirene, daughters of Zeus and Themis, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 901; Ωραι πολυάνθεμοι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.17, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 261.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.150, etc.: frequently joined with the Χάριτες, “hymnus ad Apollinem” 194, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 75}}; worshipped at Athens, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.35.1; at Argos, [prev. author] 2.20.5; at Attaleia, “BMus.Inscription” 1044 (1st c.BC).}}

5611:
** ὡραῖος**, α, ον,
**produced at the right season** (ὥρα), **seasonable, timely**: especially of the fruits of the earth, βίος or βίοτος ὡ. store of **fruits gathered in due season,** {{Refs|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 32, 307}}; ὡ. καρποί the fruits **of the season,** καρποὺς.. κατατίθεσθαι ὡραίους to store them up **in season,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.202: frequently in _neuter_, ὡραῖα, τά, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, 3.58, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 845e; ἑραίως τὰ ὡραῖα ἀποδιδόναι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.8}}; ὡραῖα.. ἀποτελεῖν ἱερά to render **fruits of the season** as sacred offerings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 116c, cf. Oracle texts cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.52; τρωκτὰ ὡ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.12; ἄνθεα “Anthologia Graeca” 9.564 (from Nicias)}}; σῦκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.3}}; also of animals, ὡ. ἄρνες **yearling,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 6.157 (3rd c.BC: Theodoridas Epigrammaticus)}}; of tunnies at a year old (from six months to one year they were called πηλαμύδες), πηλαμὺς.. ὡραία θέρους τῷ Βοσπορίτῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 503}}; ὡ. θύννοι Ps.-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.116b, cf. Hices.[prev. work] 116e, 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 38.9, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Plautus Comicus Latinus “Capt.” 851}}; τάριχος ὡ. fish **salted** or **pickled in the season,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 186.5; ἰχθύες ἐς τάγηνον ὡ. 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 6.4}}; σαργάναι ὡ. **pickling**-tubs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.27}}: hence generally, agricultural **produce,** εἶναι ἐνεχυρασίαν Αἰξωνεῦσιν ἐκ τῶν ὡ. τῶν ἐκ τοῦ χωρίου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.2492.8 (4th c.BC).}}
**2** τὰ ὡραῖα, ={τὰ καταμήνια}, especially at their first appearance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 34.}}
**3** _substantive_ ἡ ὡραία (in full, ὥρη ἡ ὡραίη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.4, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.128 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, etc.)}}, **harvest-time,** especially the twenty days before and twenty days after the rising of the dog-star, μίμνει ἐς ὡραίην till **harvest-time,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1390.}}
**3.b** the **campaigning-season,** during which the troops kept the field, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.48, 56.30, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.16.7.}}
**3.c** τὴν μὲν ὡραίην οὐκ ὕει it does not rain **in the season** (i.e. of rain), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28.}}
**II** **happening** or **done in due season, seasonable,** ἄροτος, ἔργον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 617.642}}; πλόος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 630; χειμῶνες 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.14.1; ὕδατα [prev. author] “CP” 2.2.1}}; σκαπάνη {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.16.1; τομὴ [καλάμου] [prev. author] “HP” 4.11.4}}; ὅτε ὡραῖον εἴη when **the weather permitted,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 120.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, (ὥρα (C) B) **seasonable, due, proper,** ὡραίων τυχεῖν, ={νομίμων τυχεῖν} (compare ὥριος (A). {{Refs 5th c.BC+|111.2), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 175: ἐν ὡραί[ᾳ ἐκκλησίᾳ]}} uncertain in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 668.4 (Delphi, 2nd c.BC); ἐνιαύτια ὡ. [prev. work] 1025.37 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC),compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**III** of persons, **seasonable** or **ripe** for a thing, c._genitive_, ἀνδρὸς ὡραίη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.107, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 4}}; γάμων or γάμου ὡραῖαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196, 6.122, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.9; ἐς ἥβην ὡραίαν γάμων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 12 (ὡραίων codices)}}; ὅστις οὐκέθ᾽ ὡραῖος γαμεῖ {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 804}}; ὡ. γάμος **seasonable** marriage, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 55}}; also of old persons, **ripe** or **ready for death,** πατήρ γε μὴν ὡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 516; αὐτὸς δ᾽, ἐν ὠ. γὰρ ἕσταμεν βίῳ, θνῄσκειν ἕτοιμος [prev. author] “Ph.” 968; θάνατος ὡ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 10.3; σορός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1365; ὡραῖος ἀποτέθνηκεν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.178e; so ὕλη ὡ. τέμνεσθαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.1.1.}}
**III.2** in reference to age, **in the prime of life, youthful,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 695}}: hence **in the bloom of youth,** opposed to ἄωρος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574c; ὡ. ἐὼν καὶ καλός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.94; παιδίσκη ὡραιοτάτη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1148 (_anapaest meter_), compare “Ra.” 291, 514; παῖς ὡραῖος [prev. author] “Av.” 138}}: but not necessarily implying beauty, τοῖς τῶν ὡραίων προσώποις, καλῶν δὲ μή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 601b; ἄνευ κάλλους ὡραῖοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1406b37}}; compare ὥρα (C) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.}}
**III.3** generally, of things, **beautiful, graceful,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.3.6, LXX.2Ch.36.19, NT.Matt.23.27; ἡ ὡ. πύλη τοῦ ἱεροῦ NT.Act.3.10, compare 3.2.}}
**IV** irregular _superlative_ ὡραιέστατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 186d.}}
**V** _adverb_ ὡραίως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.8.}}

5612:
** ὠρύομαι** [ῡ],
_future_ **-ύσομαι**{{LXX|LXX.Hos.11.10}}: _aorist_ ὠρῡσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.109}}:—_Ionic dialect_ and poet. Verb, very rarely used in _Attic dialect_ (see. below), **howl,** properly of wolves and dogs, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 423, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.35, 5th-6th c.AD: Coluthus Epicus 116, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.87}}; of lions, **roar,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1339}}; of animals generally, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.973a, LXX.Wis.17.19}}; ὄρθιον ὤρυσαι Pi.[same place],{{LXX|cf. LXX.Psa.37(38).9}}; of primitive folk, either in mourning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117}}, or in joy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.75; ὥσπερ ἀπόπληκτοι.. ὠρύονται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 130}}; of the sea, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 83.}}
**II** _transitive_, **howl over,** τῆνον μὰν θῶες, τῆνον λύκοι ὠ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.71; ὠ. ἐπί τινι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 10.13.}}—The _active_ {{Refs|only in “Anthologia Graeca” 11.31 (Antip., uncertain reading), 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} (Sanskrit _rauti_ (_plural_ **ruvanti**), **ruváti** 'bellow', Latin _ru-mor,_ Slavonic _rev-ą, raju-ti._)

5613:
** ὡς**:—
Summary:
**A** as _adverb of Manner_.
**A.Aa** ὧς and ὥς (with accent), **so, thus.**
**A.Ab** ὡς (without accent) of the Relat. _pronoun_ ὅς, **as.**
**A.Ac** ὡς Relat. and Interrog., **how.**
**A.Ad** ὡς temporal, **when.**
**A.Ae** ὡς Local, **where,**
**B ** ὡς, as CONJUNCTION.
**C,D** various usages. _adverb of Manner_:
**A.Aa** **ὥς**, Demonstr., ={οὕτως}, **so, thus,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.33, +others; ὢς εἶπ᾽ 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20 a. 11 (Epic style)}}; in _Ionic dialect_ Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13, +others; rare in _Attic dialect_, and almost confined to certain phrases, see below 2, 3}}; ὥς simply ={οὕτως}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 930, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338a; ἀλλ᾽ ὣς γενέσθω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 888, +others}}
**A.2** καὶ ὧς even **so, nevertheless,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.116, +others}}; οὐδ᾽ ὧς not even **so,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7.263, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.6, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.76; οὐδέ κεν ὧς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.386: the phrases καὶ ὧς, οὐδ᾽ ὧς, μηδ᾽ ὧς}} are used in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1042, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74, 7.74; also later, “PCair.Zen.” 19.10 (3rd c.BC, unaccented), “UPZ” 146.40 (2nd c.BC), “GDI” 1832.11 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), “IG” 22.850.17 (3rd c.BC)}}; κἂν ὧς, εἴπερ μέλει σοι, ἀπόστειλόν μοί τινα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 120.11 (4th c.AD)}}; δουλεύων καθὼς καὶ ὧς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 2160 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; Thess. καὶ οὗς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).234.1 (3rd c.BC)}}; for this phrase the accentuation ὧς{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|is prescribed by Hdn.Gr.2.932, +others,compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi”; 307.16, and is found in good Mss. of Homer; for the remaining uses under this head (Aa. 1, 3, 4) the accentuation ὥς is prescribed by the same grammarians.}}
**A.3** in Comparisons, ὥς.., ὡς.., **so.. as**.., etc.; and reversely ὡς.., ὣς.., **as.. so,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.512, 14.265, etc.}}; in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 530d}}; also ὥς τε.. ὣς.., **as.. thus**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 174 - 6, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1066 - 8; οἷα.. ὥς [prev. author] “El.” 151 - 5}}; ὥσπερ.., ὣς δὲ.. (in apodosi) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326d.}}
**A.4** **thus, for instance,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.129, “hymnus ad Venerem” 218}}; ὥς should be accented in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.121, 125.}}
**A.4.Ab** **ὡς**, Relat., **as,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; properly _relative_ to a _demonstrative_ _adverb_, which is frequently omitted, κινήθη δ᾽ ἀγορὴ ὡς κύματα μακρὰ θαλάσσης, i. e. οὕτως, ὡς.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.144 (φὴ 4th-3rd c.BC: Zenodotus Grammaticus): it is _relative_ not only to the regular _demonstrative_ Advs. ὥς (ὧς), τώς, ὧδε, οὕτως, αὕτως, but also to ταύτῃ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365d, etc. We find a collateral _Doric dialect_ form ὥ (which see)}}; compare ὥτε. Usage:
**A.I** in similes, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.161, +others}}; longer similes are commonly introduced by ὡς ὅτε, ὡς δ᾽ ὅτε, ἤριπε δ᾽, ὡς ὅτε πύργος [ἤριπε] {{Refs|4.462: ἤριπε δ᾽, ὡς ὅτε τις δρῦς ἤριπε 13.389, compare 2.394}}; so later, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 84.1, etc.; ὡς ὅτε θαητὸν μέγαρον, πάξομεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.2: ὡς ὅτε}} is rare in short similes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.368: ὡς}} is followed by _indicative_ _present_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.4, 16.364: also by}}_aorist_, {{Refs|3.33f, 4.275, 16.823, +others}}; also by _subjunctive_ _present_ or _aorist_, {{Refs|5.161, 10.183, 485, 13.334}} (sometimes ὡς δ᾽ ὅτ᾽ ἄν, {{Refs|11.269, 17.520)}}; compare ὥστε A:—the Verb is sometimes omitted with ὡς, but may be supplied from the context, ἐνδούπησε πεσοῦσ᾽, ὡς εἰναλίη κήξ (i.e. πίπτει) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.479, compare 6.20; θεὸς δ᾽ ὣς τίετο δήμῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.78; οἱ δὲ φέβοντο.., βόες ὣς ἀγελαῖαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.299: where ὡς}} follows the noun to which it refers, it takes the accent; so in Comedy texts, Ἀριστόδημος ὥς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 151, compare 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 75.6}}; see infr. H.
**A.I.2** **like as, just as,** ὡς οὗτος κατὰ τέκν᾽ ἔφαγε.., ὣς ἡμεῖς κτλ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.326, see supr. Aa. 3.}}
**A.I.3** sometimes in the sense **as much as** or **according as,** ἑλὼν κρέας ὥς (i. e. ὅσον) οἱ χεῖρες ἐχάνδανον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.344}}; ὦκα δὲ μητρὶ ἔννεπον ὡς (i. e. ὅσα) εἶδόν τε καὶ ἔκλυον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 172; τῶν πάντων οὐ τόσσον ὀδύρομαι.. ὡς ἑνός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.105; τόσον.. ὡς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.130}}; so in Trag., σοὶ θεοὶ πόροιεν ὡς ἐγὼ θέλω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1124; ὡς ἐγὼ οὐκ ἔστιν ὑμῶν ὅστις ἐξ ἴσου νοσεῖ [prev. author] “OT” 60}}; in Prose, ὡς δύναται **as much as** he can, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 278; τὸ ῥῆμα μέμνημαι ὡς εἶπε 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.72}}; ὡς μή ={ὅσον μή, νέμεν ὅτι ἃν} (={ἂν}) βόλητοι ὡς μὴ ἰν τοῖ περιχώροι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).3.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; cf. Ab. 11.2 below
**A.I.4** sometimes after _comparative_, **compared with,** hence **than,** μᾶλλον πρέπει οὕτως ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36d; ἅ γε μείζω πόνον παρέχει.. οὐκ ἂν ῥᾳδίως οὐδὲ πολλὰ ἂν εὕροις ὡς τοῦτο [prev. author] “R.” 526c}}; οὐδενὸς μᾶλλον φροντίζειν ὡς.. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.12.5, compare 7.4.5, 11.2.9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 36: μᾶσσον ὡς}} is uncertain in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 629}}, and [ἢ] should perhaps be inserted in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.12,31}}; compare ὥσπερ IV.
**A.II** with Adverbial clauses:
**A.II.1** parenthetically, in qualifying clauses, ὡς ἔοικε, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 176c, etc.}}: in these cases γε or γοῦν is frequently added, ὡς γοῦν ὁ λόγος σημαίνει **as** at any rate the argument shows, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 334a}}; in some phrases with _infinitive_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.3. An anacoluthon sometimes occurs by the Verb of the principal clause being made dependent on the parenthetic Verb, ὡς δὲ Σκύθαι λέγουσι, νεώτατον ἁπάντων ἐθνέων εἶναι (for ἦν)}} τὸ σφέτερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.5, compare 1.65; ὡς ἐγὼ ἤκουσα, εἶναι αὐτόν [prev. author] 4.76}}; ὡς γὰρ.. ἤκουσά τινος, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.18 codices}}; ἁνὴρ ὅδ᾽ ὡς ἔοικεν οὐ νεμεῖν (for οὐ νεμεῖ, ὡς ἔοικε), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1238.}}
**A.II.2** in elliptical phrases, **so far as**.. (compare supr. Ab.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.3) ὡς ἐμοί [prev. author] “Aj.” 395 (Lyric poetry); so ὥς γε ἐμοὶ κριτῇ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.41 and ὥς γ᾽ ἐμοὶ χρῆσθαι κριτῇ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 801; ὡς ἐμῇ δόξῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 5.2}}; ὡς ἀπ᾽ ὀμμάτων (i.e. εἰκάσαι) to judge by eyesight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 15}}: especially in such phrases as οὐκέτι πολλὸν χωρίον, ὡς εἶναι Αἰγύπτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.8}}; οὐδὲ ἀδύνατος, ὡς Αακεδαιμόνιος **for** a Lacedaemonian, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.84, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.31; ὡς ἀνθρώποις 5th c.BC: Alcmaeon Philosophus 1}}; φρονεῖ.. ὡς γυνὴ μέγα **for** a woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1078}}; πιστός, ὡς νομεύς, ἀνήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1118; μακρὰν ὡς γέροντι.. ὁδόν [prev. author] “OC” 20, compare 385, “Ant.” 62, etc.; ὡπλισμένοι ὡς ἐν τοῖς ὄρεσιν ἱκανῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.31; also with ἄν, μεγάλα ἐκτήσατο χρήματα, ὡς ἂν εἶναι Ῥοδῶπιν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.135 codices (ἂν secl. Krüger, Ῥοδώπιος conjecture Valck.)}}:—for ὡς εἰπεῖν and the like, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.3.}}
**A.II.3** ὡς attached to the object of the Verb, **as,** ἑωυτὸν ὡς ἐχθρὸν λυπέει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 88; ἔλαβεν ἀμφοτέρους ὡς φίλους ἤδη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.25; ἐν οἰκήματι ᾧ ὡς ταμιείῳ ἐχρῆτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315d}}.— For the similar usage of ὡς with Participles and Prepositions, see below with
**A.III** with Adverbs:
**A.III.a** with the Posit., ὡς ἀληθῶς **truly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 234e (compare ἀληθής 111.1b: as if _adverb_ of τὸ ἀληθές)}}; ὡς ἑτέρως **in the other way,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 276c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.212}} (_adverb_ of ὁ ἕτερο; see at {ἕτερος} see 2) (see. below {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.1)}}; ὡς ἠπίως, ὡς ἐτητύμως, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1438 (Lyric poetry), 1452; ὡς ὁμοίως “SIG” 708.34 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC), LXX.4Mac.5.21, 1 “Enoch” 5.3, “IG” 7.2725.16 (Acraeph., 2nd c.AD)}}; ὡς ἐναλλάξ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 215.9, 340.2; ὡς παντελῶς [prev. author] 184.26; ὡς ἄλλως 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.27, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.32; ὡς ἐνδεχομένως “PPetr.” 2p.53 (3rd c.BC)}}; in ὣς αὔτως (see. ὡσαύτως) we have the _adverb_ of ὁ αὐτός, but the ὥς retains its _demonstrative_ force, as does ὁ in Homer; ὡς ἀληθῶς, ὡς ὁμοίως, and ὡς παντελῶς may be modelled on ὣς αὔτως, with which they are nearly synonymous; so also ὡς ἑτέρως and ὡς ἐναλλάξ, which are contrasted with it.
**A.III.b** with _adverbs_ expressing anything extraordinary, θαυμαστῶς or θαυμασίως ὡς, ὑπερφυῶς ὡς, (see entry).; ὡς is sometimes separated by several words from its _adverb_, as θαυμαστῶς μοι εἶπες ὡς παρα᾽ δόξαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95a; ὑπερφυῶς δὴ τὸ χρῆμα ὡς δύσγνωστον φαίνεται [prev. author] Alcaeus2.147c, compare “Phd.” 99d.}}
**A.III.c** with the _superlative_, **as much as can be,** ὡς μάλιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141, etc.: ὡς ῥᾷστα}} **as** easily **as possible,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 104; ὡς πλεῖστα 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 189}}; ὠς τάχιστα **as** quickly **as possible,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 4.15, etc.; more fully expressed, ὡς δυνατὸν ἄριστα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.153; ὡς ἐδύναντο ἀδηλότατα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.50; μαχομένους ὡς ἂν δυνώμεθα κράτιστα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.6; ὡς οἷόν τε βελτιστον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 403d}}; ὡς ἀνυστὸν κάλλιστα Diog.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus 3: ὡς and ὅτι}} are sometimes found together, where one is superfluous, ὡς ὅτι μάλιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 908a; βοῦν ὡς ὅτι κάλλιστον “IG” 22.1028.17 (2nd-1st c.BC)}}; see infr. G.
**A.III.d** with _comparative_, ὡς θᾶσσον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.66.1, 3.82.1.}}
**A.III.e** in the phrases ὡς τὸ πολύ, ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c, 377b}}; ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πλεῖον for the more part, commonly, ὡς ἐπὶ πλεῖστον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34}}; ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πλῆθος, ὡς πλήθει, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 364a, 389d; ὡς τὸ ἐπίπαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50, etc.; ὡς τὰ πολλά 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.17.}}
**A.III.2** with _adjectives_,
**A.III.2.a** Posit., ὑπερφυεῖ τινι.. ὡς μεγάλῃ βλάβῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 477d.}}
**A.III.2.b** with _superlative_, ὡς ἄριστοι τὰς φύσεις {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 18d; ὅπως ὡς βέλτισται ἔσονται [prev. author] “Grg.” 503a; ὡς ὅτι βέλτιστον [prev. author] “Smp.” 218d.}}
**A.III.2.c** separated from the _adjective_ by a _preposition_, ὡς ἐς ἐλάχιστον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.63, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.246; ὡς ἐν βραχυτάτοις 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.18; ὡς ἐν ἐχυρωτάτῳ ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.26, etc.}}
**A.III.2.Ac** Relat. and Interrog., **how,** μερμήριζε.. ὡς Ἀχιλῆα τιμήσειε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365a; ἐβουλεύοντο ὡς.. στήσονται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.84, etc.; οἷα δεῖ λέγειν καὶ ὥς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1128a1}}; ὡς πέπραται **how,** i. e. **at what price** the goods have been sold, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 149 (3rd c.BC)}}; so οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὡς.. (for the more usually ὅπως) nowise can it be **that**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 750}}; οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὡς οὐ.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 196 (_anapaest meter_, Porson for οὐκ ἔστιν ὅπως ου)}}; οἶσθ᾽ ὡς πόησο; by a mixture of constructions for ὡς χρὴ ποιῆσαι or ὡς ποιήσεις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 543, compare 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 43, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 916}}; οἶσθ᾽ ὡς μετεύξει is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 600 (μέτευξαι Elmsley)}}; similarly, οἶσθα.. ὡς νῦν μὴ σφαλῇς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 75.}}
**A.III.2** ὡς ἂν ποήσῃς **however** (**in whatever way**) thou mayest act, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1369, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 181a; αὐτῷ ὥς κεν ἅδῃ, τὼς ἔσσεται 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.350.}}
**A.III.2.Ad** Temporal, **when,** with past tenses of the _indicative_, ἐνῶρτο γέλως.., ὡς ἴδον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.600}}: with _optative_, to express a repeated action, **whenever,** ὡς.. ἐς τὴν Μιλησίην ἀπίκοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17}}: rarely with _subjunctive_, to denote what happens under certain conditions, τῶν δὲ ὡς ἕκαστός οἱ μειχθῇ, διδοῖ δῶρον {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.172, compare 1.132}}; later, ὡς ἄν with _subjunctive_, **when,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 251 (3rd c.BC), NT.1Cor.11.34, etc.; ὥς κα “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.170 (from Cyrene)}}; ὡς ἂν τάχιστα λάβῃς τὴν ἐπιστολήν **as** soon **as..** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 241.1 (3rd c.BC),cf. LXX.1Ki.9.13, LXX.Lev.3.8}}: in indirect speech with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, 96, +others: expressed more forcibly by ὡς.. τάχιστα, some word or words being interposed, ὡς γὰρ ἐπετρόπευσε τάχιστα}} **as soon as ever**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.65; ὡς δὲ ἀφίκετο τάχιστα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.2: less frequently ὡς τάχιστα}} stand together, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.22}}: but this usage must be distinguished from signf. Ab.111.1c: followed by _demonstrative_, ὡς εἶδ᾽, ὣς ἀνεπᾶλτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.424; ὡς ἴδεν, ὥς μιν ἔρως πυκινὰς φρένας ἀμφεκάλυψεν 14.294; also ὡς.., ἔπειτα 3.396; Κρονίδης ὥς μιν φράσαθ᾽ ὣς ἐόλητο θυμὸν ἀνωΐστοισιν ὑποδμηθεὶς βελέεσσι Κύπριδος 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.74}}; the second ὣς is repeated, ἁ δ᾽ Ἀταλάντα ὡς ἴδεν, ὣς ἐμάνη, ὣς ἐς βαθὺν ἅλατ᾽ ἔρωτα [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.41 (ὣς ={εὐθέως}, Scholia vet.), compare 2.82; in 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.40 the clauses with ὡς all belong to the protasis.}}]
**A.III.2** ὡς appears to be _falsa lectio_ for{ἕως} in ὡς ἂν αὑτὸς ἥλιος.. αἴρῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1330, ὡς ἂν ᾖς οἷόσπερ εἶ [prev. author] “Aj.” 1117; compare ὥσπερ 111.1}}: but in later Gr. ={ἕως}, **while,** ὡς τὸ φῶς ἔχετε {{NT|NT.John.12.35, 36; ὡς καιρὸν ἔχομεν NT.Gal.6.10, compare “epigram Gr.” 646a5 (p.529)}}; also **until,** τίθεται ἐπὶ ἀνθράκων ὡς ἀναξηρανθῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PLeid.X.” 89 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; ἔα ἀφρίζειν τὴν πίσσαν ὡς οὗ ἐκλείπῃ {{Refs|[prev. work]37B.}};compare [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Etymologicum Magnum” 824.43 (conversely ἕως for ὡς final, see at {ἕως} (B) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.4).}}]
**A.III.2.Ae** Local, **where,** in dialects, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.13, 5.101, 103, “IG” 9(2).205.4 (Melitea, 3rd c.BC), “SIG” 685.63, +others (Cretan, 2nd c.BC), “IG” 12(1).736.5 (from Camirus), “GDI” 5597.8 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC).}}
**B** **ὡς** as CONJUNCTION:
**B.I** with Substantive clauses, to express a fact, ={ὅτι}, **that.**
**B.II** with Final clauses, to express an end or purpose, ={ἵνα, ὅπως}, **so that, in order that.**
**B.III** Consecutive, ={ὥστε}, **so that.**
**B.IV** Causal, **since, because.**
**B.I** with Substantive Clauses, with verbs of learning, saying, etc., **that,** expressing a fact, γνωτὸν.., ὡς ἤδη Τρώεσσιν ὀλέθρου πείρατ᾽ ἐφῆπται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.402, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.194, etc.: in commands, προεῖπεν ὡς μηδεὶς κινήσοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.22}}: with Verbs of fear or anxiety, with _future_ _indicative_, μηκέτ᾽ ἐκφοβοῦ, μητρῷον ὥς σε λῆμ᾽ ἀτιμάσει ποτέ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1427, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.30}}; μὴ φοβοῦ ὡς ἀπορήσεις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.2.12, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.36}}; a sentence beginning with ὡς is sometimes, when interrupted, resumed by ὅτι, and vice versa, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 470d, “Hp.Ma.” 281c}}; so ὡς with a finite Verb passes into the _accusative_ and _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.70, 8.118: both constructions mixed in the same clause, ἐλογίζετο ὡς.. ἧττον ἂν αὐτοὺς ἐθέλειν.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.25, compare “HG” 3.4.27: after primary tenses (including historic _present_) ὡς}} is followed by _indicative_, after historic tenses by _optative_ (sometimes by _indicative_, both constructions in ὑπίσχοντο.. ἀμυνέειν, φράζοντες ὡς οὔ σφι περιοπτέη ἐστὶ ἡ Ἑλλὰς ἀπολλυμένη.. ἀλλὰ τιμωρητέον εἴη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.168)}}: sometimes with _optative_ after a primary tense, κατάπτονται.. λέγοντες ὡς Ἀρίστων.. οὐ φήσειε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.69, compare 1.70, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 156b.}}
**B.I.2** with Verbs of feeling, χαίρει δέ μοι ἦτορ, ὥς μευ ἀεὶ μέμνησαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.648; ἄχος ἔλλαβ᾽ Ἀχαιοὺς ὡς ἔπεσ᾽ 16.600.}}
**B.II** with Final Clauses, **that, in order that**; in this sense ὡς and ὡς ἄν, _Epic dialect_ ὥς κεν, are used with the _subjunctive_ after primary tenses of the _indicative_, and with the _optative_ after the past tenses, βουλὴν ὑποθησόμεθ᾽.., ὡς μὴ πάντες ὄλωνται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.37; τύμβον χεύαμεν.., ὥς κεν τηλεφανὴς.. εἴη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.83; ἡμεῖς δ᾽ ἴωμεν ὡς, ὁπηνίκ᾽ ἂν θεὸς πλοῦν ἡμὶν εἴκῃ, τηνικαυθ᾽ ὁρμώμεθα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 464; [νέας] διηκοσίας περιέπεμπον.. ὡς ἂν μὴ ὀφθείησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.7. b. rarely with}}_future_ _indicative_, ὡς μὴ ὦν αὐτοὶ τε ἀπολέεσθε (conjecture Cobet for ἀπόλεσθε) κἀμὲ τρώσετε, ἐς ἄλλον τινὰ δῆμον ἀποίχεσθε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 30J.}}
**B.II.2** ὡς is also used with past tenses of the _indicative_ to express a purpose which has not been or cannot be fulfilled, τί μ᾽ οὐκ ἔκτεινας, ὡς ἔδειξα μήποτε..; **so that I** never **should**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1392; ἔδει τὰ ἐνέχυρα λαβεῖν, ὡς μηδ᾽ εἰ ἐβούλετο ἐδύνατο ἐξαπατᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.23; compare ἵνα 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.3, ὅπως 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.3.}}
**B.II.3** ὡς with _infinitive_, to limit an assertion, ὡς μὲν ἐμοὶ δοκέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.95, compare 2.124; ὡς εἰπεῖν λόγῳ [prev. work] 53}}; or ὡς ἔπος εἰπεῖν,compare ἔπος {{Refs|11.4}}; ὡς συντόμως, or ὡς συνελόντι εἰπεῖν **to** speak shortly, **to** be brief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 12.19, “Mem.” 3.8.10}}; ὡς εἰκάσαι **to** make a guess, i.e. probably, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, etc.; ὡς μικρὸν μεγάλῳ εἰκάσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.36 (so without ὡς, οὐ πολλῷ λόγῳ εἰπεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61), see supr. Ab. 11.1, 2.}}
**B.III** to express Consequence, like{ὥστε}, **so that,** frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εὖρος ὡς δύο τριήρεας πλέειν ὁμοῦ}} in breadth **such that** two triremes could sail abreast, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.24; ὑψηλὸν οὕτω.., ὡς τὰς κορυφὰς αὐτοῦ οὐκ οἷά τε εἶναι ἰδέσθαι 4.184}}; so in Trag. and Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 437, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 84, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7, etc.; ἀπέχοντας ἀπ᾽ ἀλλάλων ὡς ἦμεν Ϝικατίπεδον ἄντομον “Tab.Heracl.” 1.75; οὕτως.. ὡς ὁμολογεῖν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.164d; ὡς καὶ τοὺς τεχνίτας λανθάνειν “PHolm.” 9.13}}; also, like{ὥστε}, with Indic., οὕτω κλεινὴ ἐγένετο, ὡς.. ἐξέμαθον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.135, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 590, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.33.}}
**B.III.2** ἢ ὡς after a _comparative_, μάσσον᾽ ἢ ὡς ἰδέμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.113; μαλακώτεροι.. ἢ ὡς κάλλιον αὐτοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 410d; compare ὥστε 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.2: with words implying comparison, ὀλίγοι ἐσμὲν ὡς ἐγκρατεῖς εἶναι αὐτῶν}} too few **to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.15, γραῦς εἶ, ὦ Ἐλπινίκη, ὡς τηλικαῦτα διαπράττεσθαι πράγματα}} too old **to**.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Stesimbrotus Historicus 5J.}}
**B.III.3** ὡς is sometimes omitted where the antecedent demonstrative is expressed, οὕτω ἰσχυραί, μόγις ἂν διαρρήξειας so strong, you could hardly break them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.12; ῥώμη σώματος τοιήδε, ἀεθλοφόροι τε ἀμφότεροι ἦσαν [prev. author] 1.31.}}
**B.IV** Causal, **inasmuch as, since,** τί ποτε λέγεις, ὦ τέκνο; ὡς οὐ μανθάνω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 914, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 843, 1077, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 278: with _optative_, μὴ καὶ λάθῃ με προσπεσών· ὡς μᾶλλον ἂν ἕλοιτο μ᾽ ἢ τοὺς πάντας Ἀργείους λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 46.}}
**B.IV.2** **on the ground that**.., with _future_ _indicative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.27.}}
**C** **ὡς** before
**C.I** Participles;
**C.II** Prepositions; and
**C.III** ὡς itself as a Preposition.
**C.IV** with Participles in the case of the Subject, to mark the reason or motive of the action, **as if, as,** ὡς οὐκ ἀΐοντι ἐοικώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.430 (see. infr. G)}}; ἀγανακτοῦσιν ὡς μεγάλων τινῶν ἀπεστερημένοι (i. e. ἡγούμενοι μεγάλων τινῶν ἀπεστερῆσθαι), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 329a}}: most frequently with _participle_ _future_, διαβαίνει.., ὡς ἀμήσων τὸν σῖτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.28, compare 91; παρεσκευάζοντο ὡς πολεμήσοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.7, etc.; δηλοῖς ὥς τι σημανῶν νέον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 242; ὡς τεθνήξων ἴσθι νυνί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325 (troch.): in questions, παρὰ Πρωταγόραν νῦν ἐπιχειρεῖς ἰέναι, ὡς παρὰ τίνα ἀφιξόμενο; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311b; ὡς τί δὴ θέλω; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 557}}; with _verbs_ of knowing, ἐπιστάσθω Κροῖσος ὡς ὕστερον.. ἁλοὺς τῆς πεπρωμένης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91}}; ὡς μὴ 'μπολήσων ἴσθι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1063.}}
**C.IV.2** with Participles in oblique cases, λέγουσιν ἡμᾶς ὡς ὀλωλότας they speak of us **as** dead, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 672; ὡς μηδὲν εἰδότ᾽ ἴσθι μ᾽ ὧν ἀνιστορεῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 253; τὸν ἐκβαίνοντα κολάζουσιν ὡς παρανομοῦντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 338e; ἵνα μὴ ἀγανακτῇ ὑπὲρ ἐμοῦ ὡς δεινὰ πάσχοντος [prev. author] “Phd.” 115e, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.85, 9.54; νῦν δέ σου τὰ ἔργα φανερὰ γεγένηται οὐχ ὡς ἀνιωμένου ἀλλ᾽ ὡς ἡδομένου τοῖς γιγνομένοις 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.32; κτύπος φωτὸς ὡς τειρομένου [του] 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 202 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν ὀλιγωρίᾳ ἐποιοῦντο, ὡς, ὅταν ἐξέλθωσιν, ἢ οὐχ ὑπομενοῦντας σφᾶς ἢ ῥᾳδίως ληψόμενοι βίᾳ made light of the matter, **in the belief that**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.5.}}—Both constructions in one sentence, τοὺς κόσμους εἴασε χαίρειν ὡς ἀλλοτρίους τε ὄντας καὶ πλέον θάτερον ἡγησάμενος ἀπεργάζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.9.}}
**C.IV.3** with Parts. put _absolutely_ in _genitive_, νῦν δέ, ὡς οὕτω ἐχόντων, στρατιὴν ἐκπέμπετε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144}}; ἐρώτα ὅτι βούλει, ὡς τἀληθῆ ἐροῦντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.9; ὡς ὧδ᾽ ἐχόντων τῶνδ᾽ ἐπίστασθαί σε χρή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 281, compare 904, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 760, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1311, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.6}}: so also in _accusative_, μισθὸν αἰτοῦσιν, ὡς οὐχὶ αὐτοῖσιν ὠφελίαν ἐσομένην ἐκ τοῦ ἄρχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 345e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1461}}: with both cases in one sentence, ὡς καὶ τῶν Ἀθηναίων προσδοκίμων ὄντων ἄλλῃ στρατιᾷ καὶ.. διαπεπολεμησόμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604b.}}
**C.II** ὡς before Preps., ἀνήγοντο ὡς ἐπὶ ναυμαχίαν (variant{-ίᾳ}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.48, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.22; φρύγανα συλλέγοντες ὡς ἐπὶ πῦρ [prev. author] “An.” 4.3.11}}; κατέλαβε τὴν ἀκρόπολιν ὡς ἐπὶ τυραννίδι, expressing the purpose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126; ἀπέπλεον.. ὡς ἐς τὰς Ἀθήνας [prev. author] 6.61; πλεῖς ὡς πρὸς οἶκον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 58; τὸ βούλευμ᾽ ὡς ἐπ᾽ Ἀργείοις τόδ᾽ ἦν [prev. author] “Aj.” 44: in these passages ὡς}} marks an intention; not so in the following: ἀπαγγέλλετε τῇ μητρὶ [χαίρειν] ὡς παρ᾽ ἐμοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.28; also ὡς ἀπὸ τῆς πομπῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327c; ὡς ἐκ κακῶν ἐχάρη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.101.}}
**C.II.b** later, in geographical expressions, of direction, προϊών, ὡς ἐπὶ τὸν Πηνειόν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.5.8, compare 13.1.22; ὡς πρὸς ἕω βλέπων [prev. author] 8.6.1, compare 7.6.2}}; ὡς εἰς Φηραίαν (to be read Ἡραίαν) ἰόντων {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.3.32.}}
**C.III** ὡς as a _preposition_, properly in cases where the object is a person, not a place: once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ὡς αἰεὶ τὸν ὁμοῖον ἄγει θεὸς ὡς τὸν ὁμοῖον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.218}} (variant{ἐς τὸν ὁμοῖον},compare αἶνος Ὁμηρικός, αἰὲν ὁμοῖον ὡς θεός.. ἐς τὸν ὁμοῖον ἄγει {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 1.1.10}}; ἔρχεται.. ἕκαστον τὸ ὅμοιον ὡς τὸ ὅ., τὸ πυκνὸν ὡς τὸ πυκνόν κτλ. (with variant{ἐς}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 17), but possibly ὡς.. ὥς}} **as.. so,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” [same place]; also in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἐσελθεῖν ὡς τὴν θυγατέρα 2.121}}.έ: frequently in _Attic dialect_, ὡς Ἆγιν ἐπρεσβεύσαντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.5, etc.; ἀφίκετο ὡς Περδίκκαν καὶ ἐς τὴν Χαλκιδικήν [prev. author] 4.79; ἀπέπλευσαν ἐς Φώκαιαν.. ὡς Ἀστύοχον [prev. author] 8.31}}; ναῦς ἐς τὸν Ἑλλήσποντον ὡς Φαρνάβαζον ἀποπέμπειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 39; ὡς ἐκεῖνον πλέομεν ὥσπερ πρὸς δεσπότην 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.121}}; the examples of ὡς with names of places are corrupt, e.g. ὡς τὴν Μίλητον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.36 (ἐς codex Vat.)}}; ὡς Ἄβυδον one Ms. in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.103; ὡς τὸ πρόσθεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 242: in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1481 ὡς τὰς ἀδελφὰς.. τὰς ἐμὰς χέρας}} is equivalent to ὡς ἐμὲ τὸν ἀδελφό; in {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 366 δόμους ὡς τούσδε house = household.}}
**D** **ὡς** in independent sentences:
**D.I** as an exclamation, **how,** mostly with _adverbs_ and _adjectives_, ὡς ἄνοον κραδίην ἔχες **how** silly a heart hadst thou! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.441}}; ὡς ἀγαθὸν καὶ παῖδα λιπέσθαι **how** good is it.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.196, compare 24.194; φρονεῖν ὡς δεινόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 316}}; ὡς ἀστεῖος ὁ ἄνθρωπος **how** charming he is! {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116d; ὡς ἐμεγαλύνθη τὰ ἔργα σου, Κύριε LXX.Psa.91(92).6, 103(104).24}}; in indirect clauses, ἐθαύμασα τοῦτο, ὡς ἡδέως.. ἀπεδέξατο marvelled **at seeing how**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89a.}}
**D.I.2** with Verbs, ὥς μοι δέχεται κακὸν ἐκ κακοῦ αἰεί **how** constantly.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.290, compare 21.273}}; ὡς οὐκ ἔστι χάρις μετόπισθ᾽ εὐεργέων **how** little thanks remain! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.319}}; ὡς ὄχλος νιν.. ἀμφέπει **see how**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 148}}; ὡς ὑπερδέδοικά σου **how greatly**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 82; so perhaps ὡς οἰμώξεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 279; ὡς ἅπανθ᾽ ὑμῖν τυραννίς ἐστι [prev. author] “V.” 488 (troch.).}}
**D.II** to mark a wish, **oh that!** with _optative_ alone, ὡς ἔρις.. ἀπόλοιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.107; ὡς ἀπόλοιτο καὶ ἄλλος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.47, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 126 (Lyric poetry)}}; also ὡς ἄν or κε with _optative_, ὡς ἂν ἔπειτ᾽ ἀπὸ σεῖο οὐκ ἐθέλοιμι λείπεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.444; ὥς κέ οἱ αὖθι γαῖα χάνοι 6.281.}}
**D.II.2** joined with other words of wishing, ὡς ὤφελες αὐτόθ᾽ ὀλέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.428; ὡς δὴ μὴ ὄφελον νικᾶν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.548.}}
**E** **ὡς** with numerals marks that they are to be taken only as a round number, **as it were, about, nearly,** σὺν ἀνθρώποις ὡς εἴκοσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.5}}; also ὡς πέντε μάλιστά κῃ **about** five (see. μάλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|111.5), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.30}}:—also with words compounded with numerals, δέπας.. ὡς τριλάγυνον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Stesichorus Lyricus 7}}; παῖς ὡς ἑπτέτης of **some** seven years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 471c; δρέπανα ὡς διπήχη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.30, compare “An.” 5.4.12; compare ὡσεί 111.}}
**F** **ὡς** in some elliptical (or apparently elliptical) phrases:
**F.1** ὡς τί δὴ τόδε (i.e. γένηται); **to what end?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 796 (troch.); compare ἵνα 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.3c.}}
**F.2** **know that** (i.e. ἴσθι), ὡς ἔστιν ἀνδρὸς τοῦδε τἄργα ταῦτά σοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 39; ὡς τοῦτό γ᾽ ἔρξας δύο φέρει δωρήματα [prev. author] “Ph.” 117; ὡς τῆσδ᾽ ἑκοῦσα παιδὸς οὐ μεθήσομαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 400, compare “Med.” 609, “Ph.” 720; ὡς τάχ᾽ οὐκέθ᾽ αἱματηρὸν.. ἀργήσει ξίφος [prev. work] 625 (troch.)}}; so in Comedy texts, ὡς ἔστ᾽ ἐν ἡμῖν τῆς πόλεως τὰ πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 32, compare 499 (_anapaest meter_), “Ach.” 333 (troch.), “Nu.” 209; also ἀλλ᾽ ὡς ἀπὸ τοῦ τείχους πάρεστιν ἄγγελος οὐδείς [prev. author] “Av.” 1119.}}
**F.3** ὡς ἕκαστος, ἕκαστοι, each **severally** (whether in respect of time, place, or other difference), ξυνελέγοντο.. Κορίνθιοι δισχίιοι ὁπλῖται, οἱ δ᾽ ἄλλοι ὡς ἕκαστοι, Φλειάσιοι δὲ πανστρατιᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.57, compare 1.107, 113}}; πρώτη τε αὕτη πόλις ξυμμαχὶς παρὰ τὸ καθεστηκὸς ἐδουλώθη, ἔπειτα δὲ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὡς ἑκάστη [ξυνέβη] (ξ. secl. Krüger: ἀπὸ κοινοῦ ἐδουλώθη {{Refs|Scholia [same place]) [prev. author] 1.98; ἄλλοι τε παριόντες ἐγκλήματα ἐποιοῦντο ὡς ἕκαστοι [prev. work] 67, compare 7.65}}; χρησμολόγοι τε ᾖδον χρησμοὺς παντοίους, ὧν ἀκροᾶσθαι ὡς ἕκαστος ὥρμητο, i. e. different persons ran to listen to different prophecies, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.21}}; τὰς ἄλλας ὡς ἑκάστην ποι ἐκπεπτωκυῖαν ἀναδησάμενοι ἐκόμιζον ἐς τὴν πόλιν they made fast to the rest **wherever each** (ship) had been run ashore, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.74}}; οἱ δ᾽ οὖν ὡς ἕκαστοι Ἕλληνες κατὰ πόλεις τε ὅσοι ἀλλήλων ξυνίεσαν καὶ ξύμπαντες ὕστερον κληθέντες οὐδὲν πρὸ τῶν Τρωικῶν.. ἁθρόοι ἔπραξαν the **various peoples** that were later called by the common name of Greeks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.3; ὡς ἑκάστῳ ἔργον προστάσσων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114}}; ὡς ἑκάστην (**one by one**) αἱρέοντες (i.e. τὰς νήσους) οἱ βάρβαροι ἐσαγήνευον τοὺς ἀνθρώπους {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 6.31, compare 79; ὡς ἑκασταχόσε 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 41.9, +others}}; rarely with a Verb, ὡς ἕκαστος ἀπικνέοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.2: later ὡς}} follows ἕκαστος, ἑκάστῳ ὡς ὁ Θεὸς ἐμέρισεν μέτρον πίστεως {{NT|NT.Rom.12.3}}:—for the etymology see infr. H; also ὡς ἑκάτεροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.74 (see. infr. H).}}
**G** **ὡς** _pleonastic_ in ὡς ὅτι {{NT+8th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.14, NT.2Cor.11.21, Scholia A 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.264, 129, 396, 3.280, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.530, uncertain reading in 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.57.}}
**H** Etymology: this word is in origin five distinct words: {{Refs|(1) ὡς '}}**as**' is the _adverb_ from the Relat. ὅς (I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} stem **yo-**); with ὡς βέλτιστος cf. Sanskrit _yācchrē[snull][tnull]á[hudot]_ 'the best possible': (2) ὧς '**thus**' is the _adverb_ of a Demonstr. stem **so-** found in Sanskrit _sa,_ Gr. ὁ, Latin _sō-c_ ({{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}; = ita, cf. Umbr. **esoc**); (3) ὡς postpositive (ὄρνιθες ὥς, etc.) constantly makes a preceding short closed syllable long in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, and must therefore have been ϝω; it may perhaps be related to Sanskrit _vā,_ a form of **va, iva** (= {{Refs|(1) or (2)}}**like**), Latin _ve,_ Gr. ἦ[ϝ] ; {{Refs|(4) ὡς preposition '}}**to**' is of doubtful origin (perhaps from *ὠς, _cognate_ with Latin _ōs_ 'face', Sanskrit _ās_: ὤς τινα ἐλθεῖν like{τί δέ δε φρένας ἵκετο πένθος};); (5) ὡς F. 3 is probably ϝως, _adverb_ of ϝός the reflexive _adjective_, and means literal **in his (their) own way** (or **place**); it is idiomatically placed before ἕκαστος (ἑκάτερος),compare ϝὸν ϝεκάτερος {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 1.18.}}

5615:
** ὡσαύτως**,
_adverb_ of ὁ αὐτός, **in like manner, just so,** used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only at the beginning of clauses with δέ inserted, ὣς δ᾽ αὔτως.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.339, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.31, +others; also in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.215, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 102e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1386b30, +others; after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, in one word, ὡσαύτως καὶ}}.. **in like manner** as.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.86, etc.; with _dative_, ὣς δ᾽ αὔτως τῇσι κυσὶ οἱ ἰχνευταὶ θάπτονται [prev. author] 2.67; πολλοὶ συνεξήκουον ὡ. ἐμοί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 372; ὥσπερ γὰρ.., ὡ. δὲ σύ [prev. author] “El.” 27}}; opposed to ὡς ἑτέρως, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 169a31.}}
**2** further strengthened, ὡ. οὕτως so **in like manner,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 460d; ὡ. κατὰ ταὐτά [prev. author] “Phd.” 78d.}}

5616:
** ὡσεί**
or **ὡς εἰ,** _adverb_ **as if, as though**; with various moods:
**I** with _optative_, ἴσαν, ὡς εἴ τε πυρὶ χθὼν πᾶσα νέμοιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.780, compare 11.389, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.314, etc.}}
**I.2** ὡσεί τε with _subjunctive_, ἐρίλησ᾽ ὡς εἴ τε πατὴρ ὃν παῖδα φιλήσῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.481.}}
**I.3** ὡς εἰ or ὡς εἴ τε, with _indicative_, ἕπονθ᾽ ὡς εἴ τε μετὰ κτίλον ἕσπετο μῆλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.492; φιάλαν ὡς εἴ τις.. δωρήσεται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.1.}}
**II** in comparisons, **like, just as,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.59, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.36, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 290, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.1, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 653, “El.” 234 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.: with _participle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.374, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 194; ὡς εἴ τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.474}}; with verb supplied, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.254, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.112}}; ὡσεί περ with _participle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 189, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.163 (nisi to be read εἰ περὶ σεῦ)}}; ὡς εἴ πέρ τε with a noun, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 215.}}
**II.2** ὡς εἴτε **as it were,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.44.}}
**III** with Numerals, or measures of time and space, **about,** ὡσεὶ τριήκοντα σταδίων μάλιστά κῃ {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.109, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.9, LXX.Gen.24.55, +others, NT.Matt.14.21, +others}}

5618:
** ὥσπερ**,
or **ὥς περ,** _adverb_ of Manner, **like as, even as,** ζῆν ὥ. ἤδη ζῇς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1396; ἐσῴζετ᾽ ἂν.., ὥ. οὐχὶ σῴζεται {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 994}}; but the Verb is more often left to be supplied, οὔ τι κατακρύπτουσιν.., ὥ. Κύκλωπες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.206, compare 2.333, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.263, 14.50}}; ἔξεστί θ᾽, ὥ. Ἡγέλοχος, ἡμῖν λέγειν.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 303; τεταγμένοι ὥ. ἔμελλον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.93; τοῖς ἠτυχηκόσιν ὥ. ἐγώ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.1; 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently puts a word between ὡς}} and περ, e.g. ὡς σύ περ αὐτή, ὡς τὸ πάρος περ, ὡς ἔσεταί περ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.385, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.806, 1.211}}; **as for instance,** ὅταν χορὸς.. γίγνηται, ὥ. [ὁ] εἰς Ηλον πεμπόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.3.12}}; ὥσπερ differs from ὡς in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, in that it seldom has an antecedent expressed, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.487}}, τηλίκου ὥ. ἐγώ; also in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 402}}, ὣς δ᾽ αὔτως.., ὥ. ὑπέστ; but in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ ὥ. is very frequently after _demonstrative_ words; before οὕτως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 3, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 188; after it, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 475, etc.}}; ὥ. καὶ.., οὕτω καὶ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.75, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 354b}}; ὥ.., ὧδε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 276; τοιοῦτος ὥ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327d}}; αὐτοῦ ὥ. εἶχον **just as** they were, **then and there,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1235; εὐθὺς ὥ. εἶχεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.19; εὐθὺς ὥ. ἔτυχε [prev. author] “HG” 3.1.19; τὰν τράπεζαν κάτθετε ὥ. ἔχει 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus in “PSI” 11.1214a2}}; καὶ τὸν δαελὸν σβῆτε ὥ. ἔχει **on the spot,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 14: with _genitive_}} ὥ. ἔχει δόξης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 612d: strengthened, ὥ. γε}}**just exactly as,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 673}}; ὥ. καί **even as,** ὡς καὶ ἐγώ περ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.477; ὥ. καὶ ἄλλο τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, etc.: ὥ. also follows ἴσος, in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.282, μοῖραν.. ἴσην, ὡς αὐτοί περ ἐλάγχανον,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 533}}; so after ὁ αὐτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.33; after ὅμοιος, ὅμοιος ἀτμὸς ὥ. ἐκ τάφου πρέπει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1311, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.34.}}
**2** ὥσπερ ἄν with _subjunctive_, see below{{Refs|111}}; with _optative_, ὥσπερ ἄν τις.. λέγοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 87b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.14; compare ὡσπερεί 11.}}
**II** to limit or modify an assertion or apologize for a metaphor, **as it were, so to speak,** ὥ. ἀκονιτί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.73; τὸν ἐγκέφαλον ὥ. σεσεῖσθαί μοι δοκεῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1276, compare “Pax” 234; ἅμα μὲν.. ὥ. ὑπεφθόνει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 270d, “Cra.” 384c}}; in later Gk. sometimes after the word to which it refers, ἐσφιγμένον ὥ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “adversus Christianos - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 26}}; βάθρον ὥ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.33; στέφανος ὥ. τῶν πόλεων τὰ τείχη [prev. work] 42}}: frequently with parts., ὥ. ἐγγελῶσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 277; ὥ. ἐντεταμένου τοῦ σώματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86b}}; ὥ. τι τῶν ἄλλων εὐλόγως πεποιηκότες **as if** they had done, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.7}}; ὥ. ἐξόν **as if** it were in our power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.14; σιωπῇ ἐδείπνουν, ὥ. τοῦτο ἐπιτεταγμένον αὐτοῖς [prev. author] “Smp.” 1.11, compare “Mem.” 2.3.3}}; with a change of construction, ὥ. τὸν ἀριθμὸν τοῦτον ἔχοντα ἀνάγκην.., καὶ οὔτε.. οἷόν τε εἴη γενέσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 2.3.19}}; τὴν ὥ. ἐπὶ τοῦ δίφρου ἕδραν a seat **like** that used in the chariot, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 7.5.}}
**III** rarely of Time,
**III.1** ὥσπερ ἄν ={ἕως ἄν}, **so long as,** or **however long** (compare ὡς Ad. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2), ὥσπερ ἂν ζῶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1361 (sed to be read ἕωσπερ).}}
**III.2** **as soon as,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 24.}}
**IV** after a _comparative_ (compare ὡς Ab. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|1.4); οὐ μείους ὥ. χίλιοι 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 3.4; ἧττον.. ὥ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.16}}.—Cf. ὡσπερεί, ὥσπερ οὖν.

5619:
** ὡσπερεί**
or ὥσπερ εἰ, _adverb_ **just as if,** with _indicative_, ὥσπερ εἰ παρεστάτεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1201; with _optative_, ὥσπερ τις εἴ σοι.. μηδὲν διδοίη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 776}}; τὸ ὡσπερεὶ φάναι to say '**as it were**', {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 32.3}}; ὅμοια ὥ. εἰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.37}}: with Nouns or parts., **as it were,** ὡ. ψῆτταν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 115; ὡ. προκείμενον [prev. author] “Ec.” 537; ἃ ὡ. στοιχεῖα τῶν ἄλλων ἐστί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 422a.}}
**II** ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ or ὡσπερανεί (properly elliptical for ὥσπερ ἂν ἦν, εἰ.., or the like), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 479a, “Prt.” 311b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.148, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.2}}; _Doric dialect_ ὥσπερ αἰκ ἐξ ἑνὸς κελεύματος κεχάναντι they gape **as it were** 'by numbers' {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 25}}; ὥσπερ οὖν ἂν εἰ with _imperfect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420c: compare ὥσπερ 1.2.}}

5620:
** ὥστε**,
as _adverb_, bearing the same relation to ὡς as ὅστε to ὅς, and used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} more frequently than ὡς in similes, when it is commonly written divisim, and is _relative_ to a _demonstrative_ ὥς: sometimes with _present_ Indic., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.459f, 12.421, 13.703}}: sometimes with _aorist_, ὥς τε λέων ἐχάρη {{Refs|3.23: sometimes with}}_subjunctive_ _present_ or _aorist_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.474f, 11.67, 16.428, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.302}}: all three usages combined in one simile, with varied construction, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.136 - 9}}:—the verb is sometimes omitted, λάμφ᾽ ὥς τε στεροπή {{Refs|10.154: this usage of ὥστε}} is chiefly _Epic dialect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus uses ὧτε, which see)}}, but it occurs in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus (?) 27 (probably), 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.124 and sometimes in Trag., κατώρυχες δ᾽ ἔναιον ὥστ᾽ ἀήσυροι μύρμηκες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 452, compare “Th.” 62, “Pers.” 424, “Ch.” 421 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 343, “Ant.” 1033, “Tr.” 112 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** to mark the power or virtue by which one does a thing, **as being, inasmuch as,** like{ἅτε}, τὸν δ᾽ ἐξήρπαξ᾽ Ἀφροδίτη ῥεῖα μάλ᾽, ὥ. θεός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.381, compare 18.518}}; ὥ. περὶ ψυχῆς **since it was** for life, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.423; ὥ. ταῦτα νομίζων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, compare 5.83, 101, 6.94.}}
**B** as _conjunction_ to express **the actual** or **intended result** of the action in the principal clause:
**B.I** mostly with _infinitive_, **so as** or **for to** do a thing, twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, εἰ δέ σοι θυμὸς ἐπέσσυται, ὥ. νέεσθαι}} if thy heart is eager **to** return, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.42}}; οὐ τηλίκος.., ὥ. σημάντορι πάντα πιθέσθαι not of such age **as to** obey a master in all things, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.21; ῥηϊδίως κεν ἐργάσσαιο, ὥ. σε κεἰς ἐνιαυτὸν ἔχειν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 44}}; ὥ. ἀποπλησθῆναι (ἀποπλῆσαι codices) τὸν χρησμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.96: frequently in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, “O.” 9.74, “N.” 5.1, 35, +others}}; also in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ after demonstratives, οὔπω τοσοῦτον ἠπατημένος κυρῶ ὥστ᾽ ἄλλα χρῄζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 595, etc.}}; this _construction_ is found in cases where (as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.42 above quoted) ὥστε}} seems superfluous; so after ἐθέλειν, Κύπρις.. ἤθελ᾽ ὥ. γίγνεσθαι τόδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1327}}; after ἔστι, for ἔξεστι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 656}}; after ψηφίζεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.17}}; after ἐπαίρειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 581; ἐπαγγελλόμενοι ὥ. βοηθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.86}}; after words implying request, δεηθέντες.. ὥ. ψηφίσασθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.119; πεῖσαι ὥ. συγχωρῆσαι [prev. author] 8.45.}}
**B.I.2** after Comparatives with ἤ, when the possibility of the consequence is denied compare (ὡς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.2), μέζω κακὰ ἢ ὥστε ἀνακλαίειν}} woes too great for tears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14; μεῖζον ἢ ὥστε φέρειν δύνασθαι κακόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.17: but in Poetry ὥστε}} is sometimes left out, νόσημα μεῖζον ἢ φέρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1293; κρείσσον᾽ ἢ φέρειν κακά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1107 (rarely in Prose, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149c)}}; similarly with the Posit., ψυχρὸν ὥ. λούσασθαι **too** cold **to** bathe in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.3}}; ἡμεῖς ἔτι νέοι ὥ. διελέσθαι **too** young **to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314b; γέρων ἐκεῖνος ὥ. δ᾽ ὠφελεῖν παρών 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 80: this ὥστε is sometimes omitted after words implying comparison, ὀλίγους εἶναι στρατιῇ τῇ Μήδων συμβαλέειν}} too few.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109; ταπεινὴ ἡ διάνοια ἐγκαρτερεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61, etc.}}
**B.I.3** ὥστε.. ἄν is used with _infinitive_, of contingencies more or less improbable, οὕτως ἐκάετο ὥστε μήτε.. ἄλλο τι ἢ γυμνοὶ ἀνέχεσθαι, ἥδιστά τε ἂν ἐς ὕσωρ ψυχρὸν σφᾶς αὐτοὺς ῥίπτειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 374, “El.” 1316, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.35.}}
**B.I.4** sometimes implying **on condition that**.., like{ἐφ᾽ ᾧτε}, παραδοῦναι σφᾶς αὐτοὺς Ἀθηναίοις, ὥστε βουλεῦσαι ὅ τι ἂν ἐκείνοις δοκῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.26.}}
**B.II** with Indic., to express the actual or possible result with emphasis, οὐκ οὕτω φρενοβλαβὴς ὁ Πρίαμος οὐδὲ οἱ ἄλλοι.., ὥ. κινδυνεύειν ἐβούλοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.120 (perhaps delendum ἐβούλοντο)}}; ἀσθενέες οὕτω, ὥ... διατετρανέεις {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.12}}; οὕτως ἀγνωμόνως ἔχετε, ὥ. ἐλπίζετε..; are you so foolish **that** you expect.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.26, βέβηκεν, ὥ. πᾶν ἐν ἡσύχῳ ἔξεστι φωνεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 82, compare “OT” 533: frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, “Mem.” 2.2.3, +others}}; with ἄν and the _imperfect_ or _aorist_ implying a supposed case, ὥστ᾽, εἰ φρονῶν ἔπρασσον, οὐδ᾽ ἂν ὧδ᾽ ἐγιγνόμην κακός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 271}}; ὥστε οὐκ ἂν ἔλαθεν αὐτόθεν ὁρμώμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.6: ὥστε τὴν πόλιν ἂν ἡγήσω πολέμου ἐργαστήριον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.26.}}
**B.II.2** at the beginning of a sentence, to mark a strong conclusion, **and so, therefore,** ὥστ᾽.. ὄλωλα καί σε προσδιαφθερῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 75; ὥστ᾽ οὐχ ὕπνῳ γ᾽ εὕδοντά μ᾽ ἐξεγείρετε [prev. author] “OT” 65; ὥ. καὶ ταῦτα λεχθήσεται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1004a22: with _imperative_, θνητὸς δ᾽ Ὀρέστης, ὥ. μὴ λίαν στένε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1172; ὥ. θάρρει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.18, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311a; ὥ. ἂν βούλησθε χειροτονήσατε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.70 codex A (-ήσετε cett.)}}; before a question, ὥ. τίς ἂν ἀπετόλμησε..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.28.}}
**B.II.3** with _optative_, with ἄν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.16; βρέφος γὰρ ἦν τότ᾽.., ὥστ᾽ οὐκ ἂν αὐτὸν γνωρίσαιμ᾽ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 379, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 857, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 943 (Lyric poetry). b. with _optative_ in indirect speech, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.23}}; after _optative_ in principal clause, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 1.13.}}
**B.II.4** with _subjunctive_, **in order that,** in Thessalian dialect, τὸς ταμίας φροντίσαι οὕστε.. γενειθεῖ τᾶ πόλι ἁ δόσις {{Refs|“BCH” 59.38 (from Crannon); ἀντιλλαβέσθαι τᾶς πόλλιος (sic) οὕστε.. ἐς πάντουν ἐγλυθεῖ τοῦν δανείουν [prev. work]p.37.}}
**B.III** with _participle_, instead of _infinitive_, after a _participle_ in the principal clause, τοσοῦτον ἁπάντων διενεγκόντες, ὥσθ᾽ ὑπὲρ Ἀργείων δυστυχησάντων Θηβαίοις.. ἐπιτάττοντες κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.64 (assuming variant)}}; οὕτω σφόδρα μισοῦντα τοῦτον, ὥστε πολὺ δὴ (ἂν Dobree) θᾶττον διαθέμενον κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.16; ὥστε.. δέον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.1.}}
**B.IV** πόλεμος σκληρὸς ὥστε λίαν **extremely,** {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.2.17.}}
**B.V** in later Greek, followed by Preps., Παρμένοντι κλειδὸς ὥ. ἐπὶ τὸ Διοσκούριον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Inscription Délos” 316.83 (3rd c.BC)}}; ξύλον ὥ. ἐπὶ τὴν ἅμαξαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(2) 287 A 52 (3rd c.BC)}}; μόλυβδος ὥ. εἰς τὸ Κύνθιον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work]203 A 52 (3rd c.BC)}}; κριθῶν ὥ. εἰς τὰ κτήνη barley **for** the animals, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 251.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὥ. εἰς ξένια φοίνικας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHal.” 1.7.4 (3rd c.BC).}}
**B.V.b** with _dative_, **for,** χρεία αὐτοῦ ἐστὶν ὥ. Πισικλεῖ it is needed **for** P., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 241 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὥ. τοῖς χησίν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(2).287 A 45 (3rd c.BC).}}

5621:
** ὠτίον**, τό,
properly _diminutive_ of οὖς, **auricle,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.63}}, compare 62; but usually ={οὖς}, {{NT+1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.81 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), LXX.1Ki.9.15, +others, NT.Matt.26.51, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.18.18, “PMag.Osl.” 1.332.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **a little handle,** προχύτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 1.9; χωρὶς ὠτίων ποτήριον 5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 31, compare 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 1.138.}}
**II.2** ={ὠτάριον} 111, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Xenocrates Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 2.58.130}}; gloss on{τήθη}, ={λεπὰς ἀγρία}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Scholia 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 396.}}

5622:
** ὠφέλ-εια**, ἡ,
required by the metre (in iambics), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 944, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 183}}; whereas **ὠφελία** is required in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 539 (_anapaest meter_), “fragment” 78 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 576 (Lyric poetry)}}: the best codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus have ὠφελία more frequently than ὠφέλεια (although B always has ὠφέλεια in “Phdr.”), and ὠφελία}} is found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.69.24 (5th c.BC, Prose), 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 9, and frequently in 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus, as “Mus.” p.54 K., +others}}: _Ionic dialect_ **ὠφελίη** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.98, +others, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.187 (1st c.AD: Alpheus Epigrammaticus)}}:— **help, aid, succour,** especially in war, ἔπεμπον ἐς τὴν Ἐπίδαμνον.. τὴν ὠ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26, compare 39; τὴν ὠ. παρέχειν τινί [prev. author] 3.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.31; ὠ. ἀνδρὶ φέρειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 78 (Lyric poetry); ὠ. προσλήψεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.7; ἀπό τινων εὑρίσκεσθαι [prev. author] 1.31; τῆς ὠ. μεταλαμβάνειν [prev. author] 1.39; τυγχάνειν [prev. author] 6.17}}; ἐπάγεσθαί τινας ἐπ᾽ ὠφελίᾳ for **aid,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.3, compare 5.38}}; ἀποχρήσασθαι τῇ ἑκατέρου ἡμῶν ὠ. to make full use of the **assistance** or **services** we both can give, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.17; μετὰ τῶν κειμένων νόμων ὠφελίας [prev. author] 3.82, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 31}}; οὐδὲν ἰατρικῆς δεῖται οὐδ᾽ ὠφελίας or **any other aid,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 217a, compare “R.” 559b}}; καὶ τοῖσιν ἑλκωθεῖσιν ὠφελίαν (ὠφέλειαν codices, unmetrically) ἔχει {{Refs|“Comica Adespota” 106.8.}}
**II** **profit, advantage,** βούλευμα ἀπ᾽ οὗ.. οὐδεμία ἔμελλε ὠφελίη ἔσεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place]; εἴ τις ὠφέλειά γε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 944}}; τὴν κοινὴν ὠ. φυλάξαι the common **interest** of all, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.80; τίς ἂν εἴη ἡμῖν ὠ. εἰδόσιν αὐτ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 167b}}; opposed to βλάβη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus (see. below2), etc.}}; opposed to ζημία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.6; ἐπ᾽ ὠφελείᾳ ἐστί τι [prev. work] 1.4.4: with _genitive_ subjecti, τὴν ὠ. τὴν τῶν τειχέων}} their **utility,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.139: with _genitive_ objecti, ἐπ᾽ ὠφελίᾳ τῶν φίλων}} for their **benefit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 334b}}; ὠφελίας ἕνεκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 398b; ἐναντία τῇ ἑαυτῶν ὠ. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.2}}; ἐν ὠ. ἐστί 'tis **of use,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.35}}; after ὠφελεῖν,compare ὠφελέω {{Refs|1.5.}}
**II.2** **source of gain** or **profit, service,** frequently in _plural_, τὰς ὠ. τὰς ἐκ τῆς στρατείας.. ἐσομένας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.15; αἱ κοιναὶ ὠ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.62; αἱ ἀπὸ τινος γιγνόμεναι ὠ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.29; ὠφελίας τε καὶ βλάβας ἀποδιδοῦσα [prev. author] “R.” 332d; αἱ παρὰ τῶν μισθοδοτούντων αὐτοὺς ὠ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.32.}}
**II.3** especially **gain made in war, spoil, booty,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.3.8, 3.82.8, “Rev.Arch.” 6(1935).31 (_plural_, Amphipolis), LXX.2Mac.8.20}}; ὠ. μεγάλαι καὶ λάφυρα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.255b; ὠφελείας ἀθροῖσαι [prev. author] “Cleom.” 12; πολλῆς ὠ. κυριεῦσαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.36; τὴν χώραν γέμειν ὠφελείας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.80.3}}; τίθεσθαι τὰ χρήματα δι᾽ ὠφελείας to regard as **booty,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.37}}; so in the chase, **game,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.4}}; so of a thief, ὠ. ἑτοίμην καὶ κατειργασμένην ἀφῆκεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.4. (Prob. abstracted from οἰκ-ωφελία, which comes from οἶκον ὀφέλλειν}} 'to **increase** the οἶκος᾽; compare ὄφελος.)

5623:
** ὠφελ-έω**,
_future_ **-ήσω** {{Refs|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 358, etc.}}: _aorist_ ὠφέλησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.127, etc.}}: _perfect_ **-ηκα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511e, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ὠφελήκη {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 31d}}:—_passive_, _future_ ὠφεληθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.22, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 10.16, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 35, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.20}}; more frequently _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, ὠφελήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, 7.67, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 343c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.14, variant in 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.61}}: _aorist_ ὠφελήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39, 5.90, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὠφέλημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 222, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 512a, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ὠφέλητο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.60}}: (ὄφελος):—**help, aid, succour,** first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below)}}; opposed to βλάπτω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d}}; opposed to ζημιόω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.5.}} —Construction:
**I** _absolutely_, **to be of use** or **service,** τὰ μηδὲν ὠφελοῦντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 44, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 196, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 348 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.9; οὐδὲν ὠφελεῖ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87; τὸ πολλάκις ὠφελοῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.35.}}
**I.2** with _accusative_ _person_, **to be of service to, benefit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.95, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 507; τὰς ψυχὰς ὠ. διδάσκοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.23}}; ὠ. τινα ἔς τι **to be of use to** one towards a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.75}}; τί δέ μ᾽ ὠφελήσουσ᾽ οἱ ῥυθμοὶ πρὸς τἄλφιτ; how will rhythms **help** me to earn my bread? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 648: _absolutely_, ἐπὶ τοῖς δεινοῖσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 84; διὰ τῶν ὤτων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.38c}}, compare 145b: with _participle_, αὐτοὺς ὠφελεῖ προσκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 970.}}
**I.2.b** especially of a general, **enrich** his soldiers by booty, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 29; τοὺς στρατιώτας ὠφεληκὼς ἀπὸ τῶν στρατειῶν [prev. author] “Caes.” 12; compare ὠφέλεια 11.3.}}
**I.3** in Poets also (see. Thom.Mag.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|p.408R.) with _dative_ _person_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 342, “Pers.” 842, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 560, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 666, 681, “Heracl.” 681, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 421 (Lyric poetry); also in 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.38, and variant in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.23}}; the compounds προσωφελέω, ἐπωφελέω, συνωφελέω also take both constructions.
**I.4** with _genitive_, uncertain in οὐδεὶς ἔρωτος τοῦδ᾽ ἐφαίνετ᾽ ὠφελῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 436 (perhaps to be read ἔρωτ᾽ ἐς τόνδ᾽).}}
**I.5** with _accusative_ _cognate_, ὠφελίαν ὠ. τινα to **render** him a **service,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 519e, compare “Euthd.” 275e; ὠφελίαν κοινῇ -οῦνται πάντες οἱ δημιουργοί [prev. author] “R.” 346c: with a _neuter_}} _adjective_, οὐδέν τινα ὠ. **to do** one no **service,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.126, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 875 (Lyric poetry)}}; πολλά, πλέον, πλεῖστον, ὡς πλεῖστα ὠ. τινα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.30, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 679, 681, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.14.}}
**II** _passive_, **receive help** or **succour, derive profit** or **advantage,** πρός τινος from a person or thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.68; ἔκ τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 222, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.3; ἀπό τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.64, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.15, compare 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Pal.” 10}}; ὑπό or παρά τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 512a, “Amat.” 132d}}; ὠ. τοῦ νόμου **to derive benefit from**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.17 (uncertain reading)}}; τινι **by** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67; διά τι [prev. work] 13}}; παρ᾽ ἐμοῦ ὠφελεῖσθαι **to make something** out of me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.13}}; ἐκ τῶν ὑμετέρων **help the mselves,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.7}}: especially of troops, **acquire booty,** πολλὰ παρὰ τὴν στρατείαν ὠ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 10; ὠ. δι᾽ ἁρπαγῆς [prev. author] “Marc.” 19}}; ὠφελεῖσθαι πρός τι **acquire advantage** towards a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.27: with}}_participle_, ὠφελεῖσθαι ἰδών **to be profited by** the sight of a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39: with adjective _neuter_, οὐδὲν ὠφελουμένη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 550: πολλὰ ὠφελεῖσθαι οὐδὲν πονοῦντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.20.}}
